    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503968/part-time-adjunct-faculty-school-of-education-psychology": {
      "title": "Part Time Adjunct Faculty, School of Education, Psychology",
      "description": "Part Time Adjunct Faculty, School of Education, Psychology Apply now Job no: 503968 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Adjunct Location: Newark, DE Categories: Psychological & Brain Sciences, School of Education Job Title: Adjunct Faculty in the School of Education (School Psychology Focus) Anticipated Start Date: Spring Semester 2027 Estimated Pay: This is a temporary, part-time, non-benefitted position. Rate of pay is based on level of education. Spring Semester 2026: Supplemental Teaching Payments | Human Resources | University of Delaware Schedule: Up to 10 course credits per semester Location: Newark, Delaware campus The School of Education at the University of Delaware in Newark, DE, seeks a dynamic and innovative instructor to teach graduate-level school psychology courses as needed starting in Spring 2027. In-person instruction is preferred. Depending on the needs of the program, classes may include fieldwork supervision classes as well as content classes covering the following topics: Academic assessment, intervention, and consultation Direct mental and behavioral health service delivery in schools Ecological systems theory and family engagement in schools Supervision and implementation in school psychology Qualifications and Responsibilities: The successful candidate will hold an Ed.S. in school psychology or an equivalent specialist-level credential (e.g., a master’s degree plus additional graduate preparation or a Certificate of Advanced Study) and have experience working as a school psychologist in schools. A doctorate (Ph.D., Ed.D., Psy.D.) in school psychology or a related field is desired but not required. Experience teaching college-level courses is highly desired; authorization to work in the US is required. The core responsibilities of this position include teaching one or more graduate-level courses in school psychology and collaborating with other school psychology instructors to ensure cohesive, high-quality learning experiences across coursework and fieldwork. Candidates should be: Committed to high-quality instruction in a collaborative, communicative-based learning environment; Able to work with a wide range of students and learning styles; Willing to collaborate with colleagues to advance student outcomes; and Experienced with using technology to foster student learning. The instructor is also expected to hold one hour of student office hours per course per week. Please note: All school psychology courses are offered on weekday evenings (i.e., Monday–Thursday, 5:30–8:30 p.m.). Application Instructions: Interested candidates should submit a letter of application and CV. Contact information for references will be requested from short-listed applicants. Additional materials may also be requested at that time, such as course evaluations and teaching philosophy. Hiring Timeline: Application Review: October 9 until position is filled. Interviews: Fall 2026 Offer Extended: Fall 2026 Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503975/post-doctoral-researcher-center-for-clean-hydrogen": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher, Center for Clean Hydrogen",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Researcher, Center for Clean Hydrogen Apply now Job no: 503975 College / VP Area: Research Office Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory Pay Grade: 90E Context of Job: The Postdoctoral Researcher will develop automated workflows integrating artificial intelligence and machine learning (AI/ML) for the Center for Clean Hydrogen’s AEM Moonshot project. The project combines AI/ML, molecular simulation, and automated, self-driving laboratories to discover anion exchange membranes (AEMs) with improved hydroxide conductivity and chemical durability. Working with collaborators with expertise in polymer chemistry, computation, and membrane characterization, the researcher will connect laboratory automation with predictive models and intelligent experiment selection to build a closed-loop discovery platform that coordinates experiments, captures and analyzes measurements, and uses results to improve models and guide subsequent experiments. Major Responsibilities: Develop automated experimental workflows, including instrument integration, data acquisition, and analysis. Integrate AI/ML models with experiments to predict material properties and guide candidate selection through active learning and uncertainty-informed decision-making. Build reproducible data pipelines connecting experimental measurements, computational results, and shared project datasets. Collaborate with project partners to validate predictions and investigate relationships among polymer chemistry, structure, conductivity, and stability. Work with project partners to scale up promising candidates to deployable devices. Maintain experimental records, document software and laboratory protocols, and prepare manuscripts for publication. Ensure proper use and maintenance of laboratory equipment, support student researchers, and follow applicable laboratory safety procedures. Qualifications: Applicants should hold a PhD by the start date in chemical engineering, materials science, chemistry, electrical engineering, mechanical engineering, computer science, or a related field. Relevant experience is welcomed in one or more of the following areas: Programming, particularly Python; laboratory automation; scientific instrumentation control; or AI/ML for scientific applications. Robotics, active learning, Bayesian optimization, molecular representations, or scientific data infrastructure. Polymers, membranes, or electrochemical characterization and analysis. Environmental/Occupational Exposures: Laboratory work may involve chemicals and materials used in membrane preparation and characterization, as well as automated and electrochemical equipment. Personal protective equipment is required as appropriate to the experimental work and applicable laboratory safety procedures. Special Requirements: Laboratory tasks require careful handling of instruments and research materials in accordance with established procedures. Work schedules may occasionally include evenings or weekends, depending on experimental timing. With or without assistance, must be able to lift up to 20 lbs to assemble components of automation equipment. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503009/teacher-the-early-learning-center": {
      "title": "Teacher*, The Early Learning Center",
      "description": "Teacher*, The Early Learning Center Apply now Job no: 503009 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Trainers & Educators GRADE: 27E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The UD Early Learning Center within the College of Education and Human Development (CEHD) is a National Association for the Education of Young Children (NAEYC) Accredited program serving children from 6 weeks to PreK, with a K-2 after school program. The ELC serves as a site for undergraduate and graduate students from across the University community to observe, complete clinical placements, and internships as well as work on faculty research projects. Teachers in the UD Early Learning Center are responsible for developing and implementing a research-based model educational program with a full-day full-year curriculum. The ELC is a professional learning community where teachers engage in study groups, course work, and research. Under the general direction of the Assistant/Associate Director, the Teacher oversees the daily operation of the classroom program, including collaborative decision-making with classroom partner, family communication, and mentoring relationships with undergraduate UD students. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Classroom and ELC Community: Plan and implement high quality inclusive early childhood programs that support the cognitive, physical, and social emotional development of the children and are: Aligned with the goals and philosophy of the Inclusive Early Childhood Education in HDFS. Consistent with Delaware Early Learning Foundations. Recommended curriculum which includes Early Head Start Performance Standards (Infant and Toddler Programs) and the NAEYC Standards for Early Childhood Professional Preparation (all programs). Provide developmentally appropriate learning experiences that reflect the current research base for children of all abilities, family contexts, cultures, and individual interests. Create and maintain inclusive environments that support development and learning for children of all abilities, family contexts, cultures, and individual interests. Cultivate and maintain collaborative relationships with families to develop shared goals for children's progress and to ensure frequent two-way communication to maximize children's development. Cultivate and maintain collaborative relationships with Early Learning Center colleagues to maximize children's development. Participate in IFSP/IEP meetings and consultations with community therapists to support the unique needs of children with diagnosed serviceable disabilities in an inclusive classroom setting. Maintain ongoing documentation and assessment (observation and developmentally appropriate tools) of children's development and program effectiveness. Use assessment data to inform instructional practice. Conduct Parent/Teacher conferences and home visits as required by age group department. Comply with DE licensing requirements, Delaware Stars for Early Success, Early Head Start National Performance Standards (Infant and Toddler Teachers only), and all accreditation criteria established by the NAEYC. Maintain classrooms following health and safety policies of the ELC and DE licensing requirements; practice universal precautions and infection control. Practice confidentiality in all matters related to staff and families as well as proprietary information regarding the operation of the ELC. Maintain communication with Assistant/Associate Director to keep her/him informed about overall classroom operation. Support UD Academic and Research Programs: Maintain a flexible classroom framework that provides supported learning opportunities for UD students assigned to the Early Learning Center for clinical experiences, internships, student teaching, and research activities. Provide mentoring and support to UD students in clinical experiences, internships, student teaching, and research activities. Complete all required evaluations of UD students in clinical experiences, internships, student teaching, and research activities. Provide professional, detailed and timely feedback to faculty concerning UD students in clinical experiences, internships, student teaching, and research activities. Maintain all classroom records as well as records required by research projects, clinical and student teaching placements, and any grant requirements. Leadership, Professional Learning and Growth: Utilize tools as chosen by the ELC (i.e., ITERS, ECERS, peer observations) to evaluate classroom environments, interactions, and materials to inform professional goals and classroom changes. Monitor Classroom Assistant performance, recommending any necessary changes and supporting improved performance through coaching and mentoring. Actively participate in ELC Committees, leadership teams, study groups, and other opportunities to improve the program collaboratively and continually. Develop annual professional development goals and participate in ongoing professional development aligned with those goals to advance own knowledge and skills related to current developments in the early education field. Attend all meetings and trainings hosted by the Early Learning Center & New Directions Early Head Start (Infant and Toddler Teachers only). Comply with ELC Departmental Policies. Perform other related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree in early childhood education or other related discipline, plus three months of supervised student teaching or 12 months experience working with children in a group setting, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Effective written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively with individuals from diverse ethnic and socioeconomic backgrounds. Must be flexible and willing to work in a suburban and/or inner city setting that supports clinical placements, internships, student teachers and research activities. Demonstrated understanding and consideration of the differing needs and concerns of individuals with varying identities, cultures, and backgrounds. Committed to fostering a workplace culture of belonging, where diversity is celebrated, and equity is a core value. SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS: Requires compliance with Delaware regulations including applicable laws and regulations - as well as National Accreditation criteria (NAEYC). Successful completion of criminal background check, child protection and adult abuse registry checks, health examination and TB test. Must be able to lift up to 40 lbs. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503937/part-time-pnr-xray-tech-student-health-services": {
      "title": "Part Time PNR X-Ray Tech, Student Health Services",
      "description": "Part Time PNR X-Ray Tech, Student Health Services Apply now Job no: 503937 College / VP Area: Vice President for Student Life Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Health Services Job Title: Casual PNR X-Ray Tech Anticipated Start Date: ASAP Estimated Pay: $34.00 Schedule: Day. varies Location: Laurel Hall and Sports Medicine About the Department / Center: Student Health Services (SHS) is the primary student health unit on the University of Delaware campus. Situated within the Division of Student Life, SHS provides essential services and support to students and campus stakeholders to enhance the development of personal and social responsibility and to foster safe, healthy, inclusive, and supportive communities at UD. Role Description: The Radiology Department is responsible for providing safe, efficient and high quality diagnostic imaging to service our students Primary Responsibilities: Perform diagnostic radiographic imaging according to physician orders Provide safe, compassionate and professional patient care Qualifications: Current American Registry of Radiologic Technologists (ARRT) registration and certification. Current State of Delaware Radiologic Technologist license. Experience with and demonstrated competency in PACS systems. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
