    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503927/administrative-assistant-iii-bus-analytics-operations-it": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant III, Bus Analytics, Operations & IT",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant III, Bus Analytics, Operations & IT Apply now Job no: 503927 College / VP Area: Lerner College Business & Econ Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support GRADE: 27N This is a temporary one-year position; continuation is contingent on available funding. CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The University of Delaware’s business school, the Alfred Lerner College of Business and Economics, is a hub for innovation, business and economics research and career success. With hands-on experiences and a technology-driven curriculum, Lerner equips students to navigate the evolving business world and drive change worldwide. The Department of Business Analytics, Operations, and Information Technology (BAOT) brings together expertise in artificial intelligence, data analytics, operations management, management information systems, and business technology. Lerner’s Department of Accounting offers AACSB-accredited graduate and undergraduate programs in accounting. Serving under the general supervision of the two Chairpersons, the Administrative Assistant III supports the day-to-day administration and operations of both the Department of BAOT and the Department of Accounting. The principal emphasis is on undergraduate program support. Performs a variety of basic to complex administrative duties including the timely processing of correspondence and Webforms; the collection, compilation, and organization of information or data; student course registration, and generation of reports and records. Prepare administrative forms which may contain confidential information. Ensures compliance with internal and external administrative policies and procedures, determines priorities, and resolves administrative issues. A high level of independent judgment, initiative and problem-solving skills, and the ability to demonstrate a high degree of confidentiality and discretion are required. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Provide administrative support to the Department Chairpersons, faculty, and staff in both departments. Perform routine and non-routine administrative tasks as assigned, complying with established deadlines and work standards and University policies. Provide front-office support for both departments, including greeting and assisting visitors, current students, and faculty; receiving and directing incoming calls, answering inquiries about the department, college and University; and serving as the first level triage for issues related to the two departments. Request, collect and file course syllabi for each course taught and file electronically in the shared drive and save paper copy in the syllabus drawer at the beginning of each semester. Submit requests to facilities for requests for service, fix-it requests, movers, and media services and handles basic maintenance of the department copier and all the department printers. Oversee the completion of departmental copy requests and submit special requests to University Printing as appropriate. Facilitate and assist with departmental events such as Honors Day, Convocation, Commencement and others. Ensure that events are managed in accordance with departmental goals and objectives. May assist with travel arrangements for faculty, staff, and students. Maintain and organize office supply inventory; purchase supplies as needed. Distribute mail in faculty and staff mailboxes as needed. Coordinate the pick-up and delivery of mail between the department and the Dean’s Office each day. Reassign faculty mailboxes as needed. Operate standard office equipment, keep in working order, and troubleshoot any issues. Coordinate the preparation of undergraduate and graduate class schedules for the faculty through CLSS. Handle the forms and processes related to registration such as change of major/minor forms, transfer credit forms and course evaluations. Assist with student course registration and enrollment issues; report processing of major/minor data and student advisor assignment in UDSIS. May also assist with course wait list process. Assist the Department’s Academic Advisor with student registration issues and administrative assistance as needed. Work with the Registrar’s office to identify and correct registration issues. Provide student information to the Honors Committee Chairperson for the selection of awards for Honors Day. Prepare mailings for Honors Day and process certificates and Award payments. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: High School diploma/GED and three years of experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Familiarity with University programs is strongly preferred, i.e. PeopleSoft, Cognos reporting and queries, UDSIS and CLSS. Knowledge of office practices and procedures. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, including the ability to create and maintain databases and spreadsheets as well as use intermediate word processing techniques. Effective communication, organizational, and interpersonal skills. Ability to handle multiple assignments concurrently. Ability to show initiative and to demonstrate a high degree of confidentiality and discretion and problem-solving. Ability to exercise initiative; propose new efficiencies and processes; and demonstrates a dedication to learning new skills. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503934/archival-processing-assistant-library": {
      "title": "Archival Processing Assistant, Library",
      "description": "Archival Processing Assistant, Library Apply now Job no: 503934 College / VP Area: Vice Provost for Libraries Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Libraries & Museums Pay Grade: 27N Context of the Job: The University of Delaware Library, Museums and Press inspires the intellectual, scholarly and creative achievement of our community. We are strongly committed to fostering safe, welcoming, and inclusive spaces and opportunities where everyone has an equal opportunity to learn, create, and grow. We collaborate with strategic campus partners to achieve these priorities, as well as with a host of national professional organizations. The Division of Special Collections and Museums holds a broadly based collection and works in response to the growing interest in collaborative interdisciplinary research, and also supports research of primary sources, rare books and other publications, minerals and artwork. As a department in the Division of Special Collections and Museums, its holdings include manuscripts, rare books, maps, prints, photographs, broadsides, periodicals, pamphlets, and ephemera from the fifteenth century to the present. Manuscript collections span more than 10,000 linear feet and printed collections number more than 200,000 titles. The collections complement the University’s teaching programs and the Library’s general collections with particular strengths in the subject areas of the Arts; English, Irish, and American literature; African American history and Delawareana; politics; and print history. Special Collections actively engages with the University’s faculty and students, and communities more broadly to make use of its collections through its instruction, exhibition, and research services programs. Additional information about the Special Collections is available online at: http://www.lib.udel.edu/ud/spec/. Under the general supervision of the Political and Public Policy Papers archivist, the Archival Processing Assistant will participate in archival processing activities for the analog and born digital records of Senator Thomas R. Carper (2001-2025). The incumbent will assist the members of the Senate Preservation Partnership Grant team to appraise, arrange, describe, and perform preservation activities on analog, digital, and audiovisual records found in the Carper collection. The Archival Processing Assistant position is an eighteen-month grant-funded appointment. Major Responsibilities: Complete select processing activities of archival collections Perform audiovisual appraisal and reformatting Perform electronic record survey, ingest, file comparison, and data clean-up Perform file reproduction and review for access, including redaction Perform rehousing of archival collections Contribute to finding aid and other metadata work Other duties as assigned Qualifications: High school diploma or GED and three to five years of related experience, or Bachelor’s degree and one to three years relevant experience in research libraries, museums or cultural/historical institutions handling special materials and related records. Familiarity with archival standards and best practices for appraisal, arrangement, description, and preservation of archival records in multiple formats. Ability to use technologies to perform daily work including but not limited to word processing, spreadsheet, database and Web-based applications. Ability to exercise discretion and maintain confidentiality in carrying out archival responsibilities. Strong organizational skills and problem-solving skills. Strong attention to detail and accuracy in work performed. Experience working or familiarity with various audiovisual formats (e.g., VHS tapes, cassette tapes), preferred. Experience working or familiarity with electronic records, preferred. Ability to thrive in a dynamic environment where responsibilities may shift based on organizational needs. Commitment to fostering a diverse educational environment and workplace and an ability to work effectively with a diverse faculty, staff, and student population. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Nov 01 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503658/business-administrator-i-college-of-engineering-corporate-partnership": {
      "title": "Business Administrator I, College of Engineering, Corporate Partnership",
      "description": "Business Administrator I, College of Engineering, Corporate Partnership Apply now Job no: 503658 College / VP Area: College of Engineering Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support Pay Grade: 30E Context of the Job: The Business Administrator I will support the College of Engineering’s efforts to engage with corporate partners in areas of workforce development, technical services, and sponsored research. This individual will report directly to the Senior Business Officer (SBO) and will be expected to interface regularly with the Associate Dean for Corporate Engagement and Entrepreneurship (ADCEE) and Associate Dean for Research (ADR). The incumbent will interface directly with corporate partners as well as internal stakeholders such as SBO, College and Department leadership, and individuals in the business, research, and legal offices at the University. The incumbent will manage the day-to-day administrative and financial operations of corporate partnerships, ensuring seamless communication, accurate financial tracking, and flawless execution of partnership benefits. The incumbent must use initiative, independent judgment and decision-making skills in carrying out the essential functions of the position. Major Responsibilities: Partner Relationship & Data Management: Serves as initial point of contact for the Corporate Partnership Program (CPP). Independently fields routine inquiries and routes urgent or high profile partnership inquiries to ADCEE. Provide high quality customer service to corporate partners related to contracts, invoicing, and tracking the types and outcomes of benefits provided to corporate partners as part of the COE CPP. Onboards corporate partners into the (CPP), including contract signing, confirming benefits, entering partnership details in customer relations management system (CRM; Salesforce), and establishing clear lines of communication for various selected benefits, e.g., UD Career Center for jobs postings. Tracks progress of partnership, confirming timely delivery of specific benefits, and assists ADCEE in preparing annual reports to corporate partners in time for membership renewal. Interfaces with COE Sr Events Manager, Chief-of-Staff, and Communications Team to ensure corporate partners are appropriately engaged for events, newsletters, recognition, and other benefits associated with CPP. Financial & Administrative Support: Manages all back-end billing and accounts receivable associated with CPP, ensuring that membership fees are received and routed appropriately to college and department units. Works with ADCEE and SBO to track revenue, expenses, and outcomes associated with CPP, which requires navigating UD internal accounting systems as well as CRM. Assists ADCEE, ADR, and SBO in preparing budget information, billing, and financial tracking for corporate service agreements, gifts, and, when requested, sponsored research. Works closely with clients to build event budgets for review and approval; suggests cost-savings measures and works to reduce event waste and excess expenses. Manages all vendor payments; prepares line-item statement of expenses for the client; and reconciles all expenses in the Concur system. Other duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in business administration, finance, communications, or a related field and four years of relevant experience or equivalent combination of education and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated knowledge of and experience with administrative and customer relations support practices and procedures. Familiarity with basic accounting and budget management practices. Highly organized and capable of prioritizing and multitasking in a fast-paced, externally-facing role. Ability to assess opportunities for improvement in processes and workflow, make recommendations and implement approved changes. Proficiency and experience with Microsoft Office applications (Outlook, Teams, Word, Excel, PowerPoint) and, preferably, CRM software (Salesforce). Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills, with the ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Ability to verify accuracy of information, analyze and resolve discrepancies and follow-up on outstanding activities, often without guidance from leadership. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503938/information-technology-support-consultant-ii": {
      "title": "Information Technology Support Consultant II",
      "description": "Information Technology Support Consultant II Apply now Job no: 503938 College / VP Area: Vice President for IT Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Information Technology Pay Grade: 28S Context of Job: IT Client Success is often the first point of contact for students, faculty members, and staff members seeking help with technology. IT-CS focuses on helping the UD community use technology effectively and efficiently. IT-CS staff members also take the lead supporting productivity, computing applications and tools, and assist with hardware and software issues. The unit is composed of the following groups: Client Support, Ask IT Support Center, Embedded IT Support and the ID Card Office. The IT Support Consultant II in the STAR/South Campus IT Team within IT CS provides general technical support and additional support in specific areas of technical expertise, primarily to the College of Health Sciences but also to the other SCIT supported units. IT support encompasses consulting, analyzing, training, writing documentation, resolving problems with software and hardware and installing/configuring new systems and devices. The group provides various on-premises and in-cloud IT services including servers and storage, server and desktop support, mobile device management & support, print management, client systems management, and other miscellaneous computing-related services to SCIT’s units served. Major Responsibilities: Consults with faculty and staff and provides desktop support for Windows and Macintosh systems by diagnosing, troubleshooting, and resolving problems with personal computer hardware, operating systems, application software, software utilities, data communications/connectivity devices, handheld devices, and other related peripherals. Answers client questions related to the integration or use of IT devices, interfaces, and processes. Promotes client self-sufficiency by recommending IT training and other self-help solutions. Serves as technical liaison between client and IT staff when needed. Consults with faculty, staff, and/or students in specific area(s) of technical expertise. Develops and delivers client education and training in area(s) of technical expertise. Creates and reviews documentation in area(s) of technical expertise. Collaborates with IT colleagues regarding technical issues revolving around area(s) of technical expertise. Participates in projects or acts as key team member involving areas of technical expertise. Serves as technical liaison between client and IT staff when needed. Develops a working understanding of the business processes, teaching, research, and administrative activities of the supported units in order to provide effective and responsive technical support. Participates in the implementation of new technologies. Resolves more complex problems by investigating multiple resources (e.g., vendor technical support, listservs, Web) and collaborating with colleagues. Conducts technical needs assessment for supported departments and evaluates technology needs of departmental staff. Researches and evaluates new technologies. Helps develop effective technology solutions that support academic and administrative activities and comply with University standards. Provides second tier support for the IT Support Center. Handles escalated trouble tickets. Mentors student workers and more junior IT Support Consultants. Develops, installs, and configures supported software applications, computer hardware, mobile devices and other related peripherals in conformance with University standards. Transfers data files to new devices. Ensures print and file sharing services are delivered according to University standards. Educates users on virus protection, backup procedures, responsible computing, and information security. Removes spyware/viruses as needed. Participates in departmental device deployment and replacement projects, including scheduling, inventory verification, installation, data migration, client communication, documentation, and post-deployment support. Uses University endpoint-management tools to deploy approved software, apply standard configurations, collect device information, and assist with troubleshooting and compliance activities. Tests software applications, computer hardware and other related peripherals as needed. Participates actively on special technology related projects or initiatives. Maintains technical knowledge and keeps abreast of technical developments in area of expertise. Maintains broad knowledge of state-of-the-art technology, equipment, systems, and applicable laws and standards. Makes recommendations for use of specific applications on campus when appropriate. Establishes and maintains appropriate working relationships with colleagues and clients and follows University and department policies and procedures governing IT efforts. Acts as a technical resource for departmental IT professionals in UD units across campus. Works within the department’s overall strategic plan following project priorities and scope. Meets deadlines and achieves goals. Performs other related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree with three years related experience or equivalent combination of education and/or experience. Experience in a University computing service environment is highly desirable. Knowledge and experience with the operation and structure of University standard word processing, presentation, database, electronic mail, web authoring and spreadsheet software packages, networking, server hardware and software, and knowledge of current technological developments. An in-depth knowledge of specific applications and/or specialized computer peripherals. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills. Ability to accommodate various needs and work effectively with a diverse user base. Effective communication skills (oral, written, listening, presentation), organization, interpersonal, and customer-service skills. Ability to work successfully in a fast-paced team-oriented environment. Must be able to manage multiple projects and be detail oriented. Aptitude for learning quickly and functioning in a dynamic technological environment. Available to provide occasional off-hours support for large college/departmental events. Bachelor's degree in a related field and two years of job-related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience utilizing and maintaining desktop and mobile operating systems. Knowledge of advanced computing techniques in developing software and hardware solutions. Effective interpersonal and communication skills. Ability to work independently prioritize client work requests. Ability to prepare accurate written correspondence. Ability to make independent decisions. Ability to manage multiple projects and responsibilities in a fast-paced service environment. Discretion in handling sensitive and confidential information. Ability to interact effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds and with varying levels of technology experience. Ability to maintain a positive and collaborative work environment. Working knowledge of Group Policy, File and Print Services, Operating System Deployment, Patch Management, TCP/IP, and basic configuration of mobile devices. Special Requirements: Requires the ability to lift, move and manipulate, with or without assistance, computer equipment and supplies weighing up to 50 pounds. This role requires working within active medical labs and clinical environments. Candidates must be able to perform their duties in these environments. May be required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to hazardous materials when entering laboratory spaces. Valid motor vehicle operator's license and personal transportation to travel between various on-campus locations. Ability to lift and move, with or without assistance, computing related equipment weighing up to 50 pounds. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
