    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503941/technical-operations-director-niimbl": {
      "title": "Technical Operations Director, NIIMBL",
      "description": "Technical Operations Director, NIIMBL Apply now Job no: 503941 College / VP Area: Research Office Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory, Program/Project Management Pay Grade: 90E Context of Job: Under the limited direction of the Institute Director, the Director, Technical Operations is accountable for all technical operations including laboratory infrastructure, safety, and oversight of a team of individuals. In the future, this team may be working three shifts with 365 day per year operations at the National Institute for Innovation in Manufacturing Biopharmaceuticals (NIIMBL). The work involves tactical decision-making to support safe and effective laboratory operations. NIIMBL is a large-scale effort dedicated to supporting the manufacturing of biopharmaceuticals, and it is established by a cooperative agreement with the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) as a public/private consortium of a diverse set of member organizations, including industry, academia, non-profit organizations, state governments, and federal agencies. NIIMBL is a member of the Manufacturing USA network of institutes funded by the federal government to advance United States competitiveness in advanced manufacturing innovation. Institutionally at the University of Delaware, NIIMBL reports to the Vice President for Research, Scholarship & Innovation. The most high-profile activities that NIIMBL does are the execution of technical projects to advance the readiness levels of relevant technologies, assays, and methods. These projects include scopes of scientific work that generally include many organizations as partners and collaborators. For example, a project may involve experimental studies, at a level consistent with best practices found in the biopharmaceutical industry, and include scientists from several large and small companies, a scientist from a Federal agency, and a scientist from a University. Many of these projects will be hosted at the University of Delaware in a state-of-the-art laboratory facility purpose-built for such studies. These projects will involve a diverse set of technologies and approaches and address drug substance, drug product, and analytical needs by the industry appropriate to several types of biopharmaceuticals: vaccines, therapeutic proteins and antibodies, bispecific antibodies, and gene therapy vectors, among others. To accelerate innovation and support standards development, the Tech Ops team hosts and performs NIIMBL technical work that is best done at HQ because the work requires a collaborative environment and is work most effectively performed at a neutral site; this is a full-time, in person, position. Major Responsibilities: Demonstrates NIIMBL’s Core Values in daily conduct and ensures all decisions, actions, and teamwork practices align with the organization’s Business Principles. Overall accountability for the entire portfolio of NIIMBL’s technical operations, including prioritization of activities through dialogue with NIIMBL Leadership and NIIMBL Program Leaders from industry. Overall accountability for laboratory safety including compliance and training of all relevant individuals. Accountability to manage Technical Operations Resources and Assets including: Staff: professional development and assignment to projects Equipment: commissioning and maintenance of highly valuable and specialized equipment infrastructure Finances: tracking and alignment with available budget Space: effective and efficient use of available space Materials, data, and intellectual property: maintaining access control over valuable assets Accountability to manage Technical Operations Activities including: Electronic laboratory notebook system infrastructure Laboratory information management system infrastructure Relevant policies and procedures Visitors to the laboratory including tours and hosted scientists Continuous improvement to increase efficiencies and productivity Serve as high-level resource for NIIMBL leadership in the execution of NIIMBL’s technical operations, ensuring they meet the needs of the biopharmaceutical manufacturing ecosystem. Ensure compliance with federal rules and regulations in the conduct of sponsored research including conflict of interest policies, research involving human subjects, export control laws, etc. Reinforce and advocate for NIIMBL’s Core Values and Business Principles Perform other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Advanced degree in job-related field preferred and a minimum of 20 years of relevant experience required including in 21CFR58 GxP operations, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience in leading and managing a team of diverse individuals in a laboratory setting. Significant project management training and experience. Significant experience establishing and growing a laboratory-based team in an industry or government environment. Effective interpersonal, oral and written communication skills. Ability to communicate effectively with diverse individuals and groups (e.g., federal and state agencies, academic, and industrial communities) both within and outside of NIIMBL. Ability to establish and maintain positive working relationships with others, both internally and externally, to achieve the goals of the organization. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503475/senior-director-development-lerner-college-of-business-economics": {
      "title": "Senior Director, Development - Lerner College of Business & Economics",
      "description": "Senior Director, Development - Lerner College of Business & Economics Apply now Job no: 503475 College / VP Area: Development & Alumni Rel Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Development & Alumni Relations PAY GRADE: 33S CONTEXT OF JOB: The Senior Director of Development is responsible for major gift fundraising for the assigned College/unit (Lerner), including identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of major and principal gift donors for the college. The Senior Director will also manage a team of frontline fundraisers, inspiring cross-functional teamwork, and collaboration to drive achievement and close gifts. Serving as the primary development liaison to the assigned College/unit, the Senior Director partners with development colleagues and University-wide administrative and academic partners involved in business curricular and co-curricular programs. The emphasis of the position is the solicitation of gifts of $100,000 or more from individuals. The Senior Director reports to the Associate Vice President of Development and works closely with assigned College leadership, faculty, and staff. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Fundraising (65%): Work closely with the Dean or Director of the assigned College/unit to lead the development and execution of an overall fundraising strategy, establish fundraising priorities, and set fundraising goals. Manage a portfolio of 50 to 75 qualified major and principal giving prospects qualified through capacity, inclination, and timing to make a significant gift of $100,000 or more. Successfully conduct all stages of the major gift process, which includes the qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of identified prospects in the assigned College/unit or in support of University-identified priorities. Provide donors and prospects with an exceptional experience moving them to gift closure through thoughtful and meaningful communication and engagement. Lead the development and execution of solicitation plans for all assigned prospects that identify key steps toward moving prospects to a successful solicitation within three years. Acquire extensive knowledge of College and University programs and projects to develop and implement donor cultivation and solicitation strategies effectively and efficiently. Expertly develop draft proposals and agreements for named initiatives and recognition opportunities for donors. Demonstrate leadership by following and executing all stated processes and procedures. Recommend process/procedural improvements as needed. College/Unit Partner (20%): Serve as the primary DAR contact and work closely with the assigned college/unit Dean, Department Chairs, faculty, and staff on the development of fundraising strategies and donor engagement activities. Lead the coordination and scheduling of DAR and College/unit monthly fundraising meetings. Serve as the lead subject matter expert for gift officers who are working with prospects in assigned college/unit. Take the lead by working collaboratively with development colleagues who manage College-based donors and prospects with philanthropic interests. Assist as needed in the coordination of stewardship reports and activities for prospects with appropriate members of the administration and faculty of the assigned College/unit. Prepares various financial, statistical, and analytical reports for management and departmental use. Management (15%): Effectively manage a team of direct reports and support their success by setting clear direction, priorities, and goals, providing performance feedback, and conducting formal appraisals. Foster a culture of collaboration, teamwork, and achievement amongst staff to achieve overarching DAR goals and metrics. Lead by example by modeling best practices, inspiring, coaching, and guiding the team on expectations, policies, and functions. Collegiality Serve as a role model and contribute to strengthening DAR’s culture by demonstrating the desired behaviors of partnering effectively with colleagues within DAR to foster a cross-functional team approach to achieve the goals of the assigned College/unit and the University overall. Perform other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree and minimum of seven years of relevant experience (development, sales, customer relations, etc.), or equivalent combination of education and experience. Proven ability to solicit and close philanthropic gifts on the six-figure level, or equivalent experience in a related field such as sales. Previous successful experience managing and motivating a frontline fundraising team. Experience working collaboratively to develop and execute strategic initiatives and fundraising goals. Strong organizational and planning skills with the ability to initiate, collaborate, implement, monitor, evaluate, and advance strategic plans that support the activities of the program and contribute to the overall goals and objectives of Advancement and the fundraising enterprise. Superior interpersonal skills. Ability to successfully interact and collaborate with varied constituencies in a professional manner, exhibiting excellent written and oral communication skills, team-building skills, and demonstrating tact, poise, and diplomacy while working with a wide variety of personalities. Ability and comfort in meeting and interacting with individuals of considerable social status and affluence. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503915/director-clinical-education": {
      "title": "Director, Clinical Education",
      "description": "Director, Clinical Education Apply now Job no: 503915 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Health Services PAY GRADE: 33E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: Under the limited direction of the Chair of the Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders, the Director of Clinical Education is responsible for developing and managing assigned clinical experiences for second year MA-SLP students) and aspects of the clinical education component of the MA program in Speech Language Pathology. Clinical education is an integral component of the University of Delaware’s Speech-Language Pathology curriculum. The Director of Clinical Education will be responsible for managing components related to the clinical education of second year MA-SLP students and coordinating with the Director of First Year Clinical Education. The Director maintains affiliations with clinical sites throughout the region and, together with our full-time Clinical Educators, is responsible for assigning and monitoring students in externship sites. In addition, the Director will provide direct patient services, teach within his/her area of expertise, perform scholarly activity in the area of Communication Sciences and Disorders and/or participate in service to the Department and/or University. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Develops, plans, coordinates, facilitates, administers, monitors and evaluates second year clinical education activities and services on behalf of the academic program and in coordination with academic and clinical faculty. Serves as liaison between the Academic Institution and Affiliated Clinical Education Sites. Communicates to concerned stakeholders (e.g., the academic institution, clinical education sites, clinical faculty and students) pertinent information related to the educational program, the clinical education site, and health care changes affecting clinical practice and education. Monitors student clinical progress and assesses performance. Provides guidance and support as required to problem solve and discuss pertinent issues with student(s), and/or clinical supervisors/instructors. Assigns second year students to clinical sites with consideration to student learning styles, level of clinical expertise, and specific opportunities available at a given clinical site. Together with full-time clinical educators, evaluates clinical education sites through student feedback, on-site visits, and ongoing communications; routinely shares this information with academic and clinical faculty/staff. Provides feedback to clinical educators concerning their effectiveness in delivering clinical learning experiences based on student feedback and through direct observations. Performs academic responsibilities consistent with the Council on Academic Accreditation (CAA), and with institutional policy. Manages administrative responsibilities consistent with CAA, federal/state regulations, institutional policy, and practice setting requirements. Identifies and utilizes university resources to assist with meeting clinical education program needs to effectively resolve issues. Develops policy and procedures for clinical site selection, utilization, and assessment. Establishes, develops, and maintains an adequate number of clinical education sites relative to quality, quantity and diversity of learning experiences (i.e., continuum of care, diagnoses, across the lifespan, health care delivery systems, payers, cultural-linguistic diversity) to meet the educational needs of students and the academic program, the philosophy and outcomes of the program, and evaluative criteria set by CAA and ASHA. Provides clinical education site development opportunities through ongoing evaluation and assessment of strengths and areas needing further development or action (e.g., in service training, discontinued student placements). Collaborates with clinical staff/faculty to promote, coordinate, plan, and provide clinical staff/faculty development opportunities using effective instructional methodologies and technologies. Encourages clinical staff/faculty to participate in local, statewide, and national forums designed to foster and discuss issues addressing clinical education. Maintains knowledge of current trends in health care and its effect on clinical education and apprises clinical educators and faculty of any changing trends. Makes recommendations regarding curriculum to faculty/staff based on feedback from affiliates and analysis of clinical education data collected. Represents the Communication Sciences and Disorders program at community, state, regional, and national events and helps to educate the University and larger communities regarding the profession of Speech-Language Pathology. Teaches courses and supervises students in the M.A. in Speech-language Pathology Program consistent with area(s) of expertise and provides direct services at the UD Speech- Language Hearing Clinic. Performs other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Master’s degree in Speech-Language Pathology and seven years of clinical service, clinical education and/or clinical coordination experience. PhD preferred. Certification as a speech-language pathologist by ASHA. Licensed or eligible for licensure as a speech-language pathologist in Delaware. Strong communication, organization, interpersonal, problem-solving, and counseling skills. Ability to prioritize, plan and organize work independently. Ability to think strategically and to recognize new opportunities. Knowledge of educational, management, and adult learning theory and principles. Computer skills that include word processing, e-mail, PowerPoint, Excel, and preferably some database experience. Ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies both within and outside the university. Knowledge of state and federal legislative, regulatory, legal, and practice issues affecting clinical education, students, and the profession of communication sciences and disorders. Ability to initiate, administer, assess, and document clinical education program outcomes. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503916/part-time-blue-hens-pa-announcer-athletics": {
      "title": "Part Time Blue Hens PA Announcer, Athletics",
      "description": "Part Time Blue Hens PA Announcer, Athletics Apply now Job no: 503916 College / VP Area: Athletics & Recreation Svcs Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Athletics Job Title: Part Time PA Announcer Anticipated Start Date: October 26th, 2026 Estimated Pay: $15 per hour Location: Bob Carpenter Center About the Department / Center: University of Delaware Athletics proudly competes at the NCAA Division I level as a member of Conference USA, building on a tradition of excellence both on the field and in the classroom. Our mission is to create unforgettable experiences for our student-athletes, fans, and community while fostering school spirit and representing the First State with pride. Role Description: The Public Address (PA) Announcer serves as the official voice of Delaware Athletics during home events, delivering player introductions, game action updates, sponsor reads, and promotional messaging. This role helps create an exciting and engaging gameday atmosphere while ensuring all announcements are communicated clearly, accurately, and professionally. Primary Responsibilities: Serve as the official Public Address (PA) Announcer for assigned University of Delaware Athletics home events. Deliver player introductions, starting lineups, substitutions, scoring updates, and other in-game announcements accurately and professionally. Read promotional messaging, sponsor acknowledgements, and public service announcements as provided by the Athletics Marketing & Engagement staff Work closely with the game presentation team, scoreboard operator, statistician, officials, and event management staff to ensure timely and accurate announcements. Maintain a professional, energetic, and engaging vocal presence that enhances the overall fan experience. Attend pre-event meetings and arrive sufficiently in advance of each event to review scripts, pronunciations, and event timelines. Accurately pronounce the names of student-athletes, coaches, officials, and sponsors by preparing in advance of each event. Adapt to game-day changes and communicate important information clearly in a fast-paced live event environment. Represent the University of Delaware Athletics Department with professionalism and excellent customer service at all times. Perform additional game presentation duties as assigned by the Athletics Marketing & Engagement staff. Qualifications: Strong public speaking and verbal communication skills. Clear, confident, and engaging speaking voice. Ability to remain composed in a fast-paced, live event environment. Strong attention to detail and accuracy. Excellent professionalism and reliability. Availability to work evenings, weekends, and select holidays based on the athletics schedule. Prior announcing, broadcasting, or live event experience preferred, but not required. Passion for collegiate athletics and creating an outstanding fan experience. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Oct 26 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503041/business-administrator-ii-office-of-the-provost": {
      "title": "Business Administrator II, Office of the Provost",
      "description": "Business Administrator II, Office of the Provost Apply now Job no: 503041 College / VP Area: Office of the Provost Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support PAY GRADE: 31E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: Working under the direction of the Vice Provost for Academic Programs and University Initiatives, the Business Administrator is responsible for the financial and administrative operations of the large and complex university initiatives portfolio. The APUI portfolio includes the FinTech Innovation Hub, the Center for Innovation in Lifelong Learning and Teaching – which includes four units(Academic Technology Services, Center for Teaching Assessment and Learning, UD Online, and Professional and Continuing Studies (which includes the access center, non-credit programs, and Osher Lifelong Learning Institute)), and the Division of Enrollment Management. The Business Administrator interacts with and serves as liaison for the units in the portfolio, and coordinates with the business administrator in enrollment management. The person in this role will work closely with the budget office to ensure the financial health of the portfolio units and align the portfolio with university level goals and mission. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Assist and advise the Vice Provost for Academic Programs and University Initiatives on fiscal, human resource, space, and procurement matters Manage the daily financial operations of units in the portfolio, with the exception of Enrollment Management Coordinate with the business administrator in the Division of Enrollment Management to ensure the financial operations of the unit are in line with broader portfolio goals and financial objectives Prepares and administers, in consultation with the Vice Provost, accounts and budgetary matters related to appointments and salary. Monitors expenditures, performs analyses, provides projections, and processes requisitions Oversees the budgets of all units in the portfolio, including basic budgets, endowments, discretionary funds, etc. Prepares projections for the portfolio and evaluates various planning scenarios as well as prepare and interpret financial reports for the Vice Provost Develop financial models and develop forecasts to interpret financial projections for the portfolio Manages annual BTA and year-end budget reconciliations Responsibility for obligations and expenditures for the budget and prepares monthly reports, analyses and projections about revenue and estimated expenditure, with recommendations for modifications Participates in audits of portfolio accounts Actively manages the portfolio and its units’ budgets Participates in the budgeting and implementation of special initiatives Provides accurate financial information to the Vice Provost and the reporting units in the portfolio Prepares and presents materials to summarize and justify unit/program budget requests, including data gathering/reporting and trend analyses in connection with budgetary formulation and administration. Assists the Vice Provost on operational and administrative matters; reviews, interprets, recommends, and/or implements financial policies; identifies and resolves issues regarding administrative and fiscal matters; develops systems to monitor academic and administrative deadlines; regularly evaluates portfolio administrative systems and services. Ensures accurate, compliant and efficient management of the portfolio procurement activities, including Concur and UD Exchange workflow processes and transactions. Ensures proper implementation of policies and procedures in the areas of finance and operations to ensure adherence to Federal and University policies. Interprets and provides guidance regarding University policies and procedures for the Vice Provost. Provides orientation and interpretation of University policies and procedures for new portfolio unit leadership. Partners with the portfolio leadership to implement strategic operational objectives, and new program initiatives. Regularly meet with the Vice Provost and portfolio leadership actively make recommendations regarding new administrative policies and procedures or changes and improvements to existing processes; develop and/or initiate new office forms and procedures. Manage fiscal affairs of the department, including budget creation/management planning, expenditure and costs analysis. Performs other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Accounting, or other related field and five years of job-related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience with budget preparation and management, financial reporting, administrative policies and procedures, and human resources management. Thrives in a fast-paced environment and is excited to advance and share in the success of highly successful programs. Experience leading complex organizations and managing complex tasks. Experience using management systems, relational databases, spreadsheets and word-processing software programs. Effective organizational skills are essential. Excellent communication skills Ability to interpret, adapt and apply guidelines and procedures. Ability to plan, organize, implement, evaluate and modify administrative support needs. Ability to gather and analyze statistical and financial data and generate ad-hoc reports. Ability to develop, plan, and implement new strategies and procedures, and be part of continuous improvement. Committed to fostering a positive workplace culture of belonging. Familiarity with UD accounting and financial systems preferred. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503889/police-officer-university-of-delaware-police-department": {
      "title": "Police Officer- University of Delaware Police Department",
      "description": "Police Officer- University of Delaware Police Department Apply now Job no: 503889 College / VP Area: Exec VP & Univ Treasurer Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Public Safety, Security, & Emergency Services PAY GRADE: 20N CONTEXT OF THE JOB: Under limited supervision, performs police patrol work. The principal emphasis is on enforcing the laws of the State of Delaware, City of Newark and University rules and regulations; investigating crimes, traffic accidents and other safety-related matters and taking corrective and/or enforcement action as required; independent judgment is required in interpreting University policy in accordance with established laws, regulations and procedures; requires the exercise of tactful and assertive interpersonal communication skills. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Depending on divisional assignment, the following are typical: Conducts high visibility patrols of the campus, either on foot or by vehicle, to ensure the security of facilities and the safety of students, staff and visitors; prevents and deters crime by observation and confrontation of unusual circumstances and individuals; notifies supervisors of security defects; locks or unlocks buildings; Responds to, initiates investigation into, and reports crimes or incidents in violation of State law, City code or University rules which impact on the safety of the University community; Takes enforcement action and makes apprehensions as required; completes all necessary documentation including preparation of summonses or warrants, affidavits, reports, referrals and arrest paper work; notifies supervisors of all enforcement related actions; Preserves crime scenes, collects and preserves evidence; identifies and interviews witnesses, suspects and victims of criminal incidents; assists other law enforcement officers in investigations; Prepares and presents or assists in presentation of cases in courts or other official proceedings; Performs traffic direction and crowd control duties as assigned; Renders first aid; transports sick, injured or otherwise disabled persons; Assists visitors, students and staff by providing information, directions and assistance; Supervises other police officers, security officers and/or miscellaneous wage and/or student employees; Participates in training and certification sessions; Inputs, searches and modifies appropriate computerized criminal justice information systems; Performs other related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Requires high school diploma or equivalent. 30 college credits, approved military, corrections experience or COPT certification preferred. Some college work in criminal justice, political science or the social sciences preferred. Basic computer skills. Ability to communicate effectively and interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds; or any equivalent combination of education and experience which provides the required knowledge, skills, and abilities. SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS: Possession of a valid motor vehicle operator's license; Must be able to be commissioned as a Police Officer for the University of Delaware (this requires successful completion of mandatory police training in the State of Delaware or equivalent training acceptable to the Delaware Council on Police Training within the first year of employment *). Must pass a pre-employment drug screening test as well as a battery of pre-employment tests designed to measure mental skills, motor skills, writing skills and physical fitness and agility applicable to law enforcement officers and a comprehensive background investigation including driving records, financial status and criminal history; Successful completion of mandatory police training in the State of Delaware or equivalent training acceptable to the Delaware Council on Police Training within the first year of employment is required. Must be at least 21 years of age and a United States citizen * When essential for operational purposes, the University of Delaware reserves the right to require credentials necessary to be commissioned as a Police Officer in the State of Delaware upon employment. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Oct 25 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503885/academic-program-coordinator-sociology-and-criminal-justice": {
      "title": "Academic Program Coordinator, Sociology and Criminal Justice",
      "description": "Academic Program Coordinator, Sociology and Criminal Justice Apply now Job no: 503885 College / VP Area: College of Arts & Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Academic Administration Salary Grade:29E Context of the Job: The College of Arts and Sciences (CAS) is the largest college at the University of Delaware, comprising 22 academic departments, 2 academic centers, and 37 interdisciplinary centers, programs, and institutes across the arts, humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences., CAS plays a central role in the University’s academic mission, providing foundational coursework for all UD students and serving more than 8,400 undergraduate and graduate majors. CAS is home to over 520 full-time faculty who are nationally and internationally recognized scholars and innovative teachers. With annual sponsored research expenditures exceeding $40 million, the College supports significant interdisciplinary research and maintains outstanding teaching and research facilities. Supported by over 425 staff members and 45 postdoctoral fellows and researchers, CAS operates with annual expenditures of over $152 million and serves as a hub for interdisciplinary engagement, connecting the University with local, national, and global communities through research, creative activity, and public service. The Department of Sociology & Criminal Justice is housed within the College of Arts and Sciences and offers nationally recognized graduate and undergraduate programs. The Department currently consists of 23 tenure track, continuing track, and temporary faculty, 3 administrative staff, approximately 700 undergraduate students, and 40 graduate students.. The Department also services many undergraduate students, with over 60 class sections taught per semester. Under the limited direction of the department Business Administrator (BA) and in coordination with the department Chairperson(s), the Academic Program Coordinator (APC) is responsible for the coordination of departmental academic programs and a wide range of department, faculty, and staff operational needs and activities. The Coordinator interacts with and supports faculty, students, College and University administrators, and alumni to support the mission and goals of the Department(s), College, and University and to advance departmental teaching, service, and research objectives. Major Responsibilities: Manage operational aspects of the departmental undergraduate and graduate programs to ensure student progress toward degree completion through both internal departmental documentation and the Stellic degree management and advising platform. Assist graduate and undergraduate students and faculty advisors with registration and scheduling issues, drop/add, overrides, transfer credit evaluations, change of registration without penalty, registration holds, and general advisement questions, in coordination with department faculty, the BA, the College Student Success Center (SSC) team, the Graduate College, and the Honors College. Meet with walk-in visitors, including prospective students (high school, new admits) and their parents/guardians regarding academic requirements, career opportunities and University resources. Respond to inquiries, investigate, and resolve problems related to undergraduate and graduate transactions handled by the department. Interpret and explain College and University policies, practices, and procedures to parents/guardians and prospective students as well as to current students and faculty. Develop curriculum guides to address issues unique to department’s majors and minors. Manage departmental course scheduling, in coordination with department faculty and the BA. Input all course data into UDSIS and manage classroom requests to secure rooms and avoid conflicts, in conjunction with other departments. Resolve conflicts of departmental courses with each other and with courses outside the department that are part of the departmental curriculum, and monitor enrollments to determine whether more sections need to be added or cancelled. Validate grade roster and faculty workload, approve change of Academic Program Plan webforms, make faculty advisor assignments, and coordinate final exam schedules and course evaluations. Monitor progress towards degree for departmental graduate students. Work with faculty advisors to track and prepare for qualifying exams, dissertation proposals, and dissertation defenses. Coordinate with the Graduate College and resolves issues as they arise. Liaison to department faculty to edit the UD Course Catalog to reflect all changes in undergraduate and graduate course descriptions and degree programs and submits descriptions of proposed new courses and curriculum changes for approval. Participate in undergraduate and graduate recruitment efforts by advising applicants on general academic requirements, managing annual graduate recruitment events, and coordinating the Department's participation in a variety of special undergraduate admissions events including Admitted Students Days and Blue & Golden Saturdays for prospective students, parents/guardians, faculty and others. Coordinate with SSC and department Chair on graduate and undergraduate New Student Orientation. Manage and update students' files and records. Check various forms for accuracy such as grade checks, overload petitions, and curriculum changes; assists students in the completion of required forms that may contain confidential information. Maintain the department's undergraduate and graduate files including but not limited to course syllabi, course descriptions, curriculum changes, enrollment records, and schedule records. Supports faculty requests for UDSIS information regarding student academic issues and for referencing unofficial transcripts. Conduct and/or facilitate data analysis as needed for the purpose of curriculum planning or assessment. Manage daily operations of the Department including: Manage event planning for department functions and special events, such as speaker series, faculty recruitment, and convocation, in coordination with College Events staff. Direct, maintain, and process department payroll for student workers, miscellaneous wage employees, and hourly graduate students. Review timesheets for accuracy, appropriate financial information, and runs ad hoc reports as needed. Resolve concerns with College HR as needed. Provide administrative support for internal communications, including updating internal intranet sites, internal newsletters, internal mailing lists, event promotion to internal audiences, and routine correspondence with faculty, staff, and students. Liaise with the CAS Communications Team to access communications services. Provide administrative support for Department Chair and faculty, as needed. Attend and take notes at departmental committee meetings for the undergraduate and graduate programs. Assume responsibility for other operational needs within the department, such as supply maintenance and standard equipment maintenance, donor acknowledgement letters, exam copying coordination, departmental mail, and records retention and filing needs. Assist with special projects and performs miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. With the assistance of the CAS Financial Operations team, oversee the daily processing and accounting for revenue and expenditures. Ensure accuracy and availability of funding and works with the Financial Operations team to maintain compliance with accounting procedures. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration or a related discipline and three years of job-related work experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience Working knowledge of undergraduate and graduate academic and recruitment policies, procedures, systems, regulations, and standards at the University level preferred. Ability to evaluate student transcripts and/or records. Knowledge of graduate academic standards and student conduct polices governing academic misconduct and student recruitment and retention issues Ability to communicate course prerequisites, certification, and/or curriculum/graduation requirements to students Effective organizational skills Ability to interpret, adapt, and apply guidelines and procedures Ability to communicate effectively and interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds Ability to thrive in a fast-paced environment Commitment to advancing and sharing successful programs and initiatives Ability to grasp, retain, and disseminate an extensive and varied body of information; the ability to exercise sound judgment and employ effective decision-making skills Ability to gather and analyze statistical and financial data and generate ad-hoc reports. A minimum of intermediate level proficiency with spreadsheets and databases Experience with University's student records system (UDSIS), degree management and advising system (Stellic), graduate application database (SLATE), and accounting system (based on PeopleSoft) preferred Ability to manage multiple priorities, complex projects, and long-term initiatives with precision Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503880/multimedia-specialist-athletics": {
      "title": "Multimedia Specialist, Athletics",
      "description": "Multimedia Specialist, Athletics Apply now Job no: 503880 College / VP Area: Athletics & Recreation Svcs Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Communications & Public Relations Pay Grade: 29E Context of Job The Assistant AD, Broadcast & Production at the University of Delaware is a valued member within the University and Department of Athletics and Campus Recreation (DACR), upholding and embracing the departmental mission of “Develop champions. Elevate UD. Fight for Delaware.” The Assistant AD, Broadcast & Production will oversee and drive all streaming production and broadcast content on behalf of the University of Delaware's Department of Athletics and Campus Recreation. This position will be charged with increasing Delaware's video streaming online across our 22 varsity sports, cheer, and various recreation and club services and will oversee a team of student interns, one full-time position, and one part-time coordinator. The Assistant AD, Broadcast & Production will be in charge of executing the digital streaming strategy and leading the broadcast production for the External Relations area in Athletics and Campus Recreation. The Assistant AD, Broadcast & Production will work closely across the External relations staff, including with multimedia, communications, and marketing teams, as well with various contacts inside the unit (coaches, administrators and staff) and outside the unit (vendors, partners, businesses, Alumni, Development, OCM, UMS, and parallel staff members at other intuitions). The incumbent will work to serve all 22 sports as fully as possible and to manage staffing, production and run of show for all streams and lead all oversight and logistical preparation for broadcast production by outside vendor. While their primary focus is broadcast, this position should be able to help creatively and proactively ideate story angles and features in collaboration with the communications team as needed. Major Responsibilities Leadership • Supervise, lead, and develop full-time Assistant Director, Broadcast & part time Broadcast Coordinator • Oversee student worker initiative; hire, train, and crew students for stream productions. Work individually with each student to create a training plan suited to their development. • Oversee, direct, produce and coordinate the stream productions of sporting events, press events, special events, etc. • Supervise game day linear production. • Actively source and hire freelance production staff including talent. • Direct the annual broadcast budget, forecasting operational needs and allocating funds across staffing, freelance support, student employment, equipment replacement cycles, technology enhancements, and production initiatives while maintaining fiscal responsibility. • Identify funding opportunities and manage the preparation of grant proposals, resulting in the successful support of capital equipment investments, facility upgrades, and strategic broadcast initiatives. Broadcast and Streaming • Develop, staff and manage a streaming schedule for 150+ sporting events, press events and other special events, etc. • Direct, produce and coordinate the streaming productions of sporting events, press events, selection shows and other special events, etc. • Lead process of scheduling linear broadcasts on local Regional Sports Networks (RSN) and hiring linear television production crews. • Serve as the primary point of contact for all broadcast streaming operations during conference tournaments, coordinating with production teams, vendors, and conference officials to ensure seamless live event coverage • Successfully maintain a variety of broadcast equipment and be able to proactively troubleshoot technical issues when they arise in studio equipment including Ross Carbonite, Mira, Xpression, and Broadcast style cameras and Fly Pack Equipment including Tricaster TC1, 3Play, LiveBook GFX, various audio mixers. • Troubleshoot various media systems within Athletics such as Daktronics Video Boards and ShowControl. • Creatively develop streaming graphics package and broadcast assets. • Maintain and implement ESPN graphic package standards, ensuring all streaming broadcasts remain current, compliant, and aligned with network branding requirements • Take an active role in increasing volume and quality of our streams to provide a top caliber product to our fans. Help to create highlights of events and then distribute to CAA schools, website, social media channels, local media, and media requests. • Collaborate with University Media Services on equipment engineering/maintenance and production staffing. • Serve as director of football broadcast operations for production staffing and gameday priorities, coordinating personnel, logistics, and operational needs to support successful live broadcast • Maintain understanding of current broadcasting trends and style. s and assist with sharing this knowledge with the Hens All Access team. • Ensure compliance with all laws and NCAA regulations governing promotion of student athletes as well as internal Delaware rules and regulations on video distribution/ usage. • Coordinate with necessary parties to ensure that sponsor elements are properly ideated, obtained, and activated during streaming and linear broadcasts. • Recommend new broadcasting ideas to help support the creative visions of the External department. • Lead development/upgrade process of broadcast systems and facilities. Miscellaneous • Support storytelling and other multimedia needs. • Assist with proactive maintenance and support of all video archiving efforts. • Perform miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. • Serve as primary broadcast contact for affiliate conference broadcasts and external vendors. Qualifications • Bachelor’s degree and three of related experience or equivalent combination of education and experience. Degree in Audio/Visual Communications, Design, Production or Journalism preferred. • Proficiency with video production software including Adobe Creative Suite. • Advanced knowledge of live production process, camera operation, different camera and lens types, proper filming techniques, microphones, and production switching equipment. • Strong knowledge of current and emergent audio/video technologies, video board operation systems, web architecture, online design and live streaming, multimedia communications and video production. • Highly motivated, proactive, energetic, creative and positive. • Effective interpersonal and oral and written communication skills. • Independent judgment and self-motivation. • Ability to work independently with an attention to detail while handling multiple tasks simultaneously. • Demonstrates an understanding and consideration of the differing needs and concerns of individuals with varying identities, cultures and backgrounds. • Committed to fostering a workplace culture of belonging, where diversity is celebrated and equity is a core value. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503833/farm-assistant-ii-iii-carvel-research-and-education-center": {
      "title": "Farm Assistant II / III, Carvel Research and Education Center",
      "description": "Farm Assistant II / III, Carvel Research and Education Center Apply now Job no: 503833 College / VP Area: College of Ag & Natural Res Work type: Staff Location: Georgetown, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory PAY GRADE: 26N (Farm Assistant II) / 27N (Farm Assistant III) CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The Farm Assistant provides operational and technical support for agricultural research and farm operations. The position operates and maintains agricultural, irrigation, and precision agriculture equipment; performs field operations within research plots according to established protocols and field plans; assists with equipment and facility maintenance; transports agricultural equipment and materials; and performs pesticide and nutrient applications. Work is performed in collaboration with farm management, faculty, staff, students, and researchers to ensure safe, efficient, and timely operations while maintaining research integrity. At the Farm Assistant III level, the position performs more skilled and independent technical work, including precise research field operations, equipment calibration and troubleshooting, repair of agricultural equipment and infrastructure, and assistance with planning and coordinating farm activities. The Farm Assistant III may also provide operational guidance to seasonal employees, students, and other personnel as assigned. The position may be filled at either the Farm Assistant II or Farm Assistant III level, based on the qualifications and experience of the selected candidate. The level and corresponding classification will be determined at the time of hire. On-campus housing is available as part of this position and is encouraged due to the nature of the role and its proximity to farm operations. However, residing on campus is not a condition of employment, and candidates who prefer to maintain their own off-campus housing will still receive full consideration. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: (Applicable to Both Levels) Operate, adjust, and maintain tractors, planters, sprayers, combines, tillage equipment, specialized research machinery, and other agricultural equipment. Perform field operations within research plots according to established protocols and field plans to maintain plot integrity and research quality. Operate precision agriculture technologies, including GPS/GNSS guidance, autosteer, rate and section control, machine monitoring, computerized equipment controls, and related technologies. Operate and maintain irrigation systems, pumps, wells, valves, controls, and related infrastructure. Perform maintenance and repairs to agricultural equipment, vehicles, irrigation systems, buildings, and other farm infrastructure. Perform preventive maintenance and maintain required equipment, maintenance, application, and research records. Assist with equipment setup, calibration, adjustment, and preparation for research and production activities. Transport agricultural equipment, materials, and supplies using light- and heavy-duty trucks and trailers. Apply pesticides, fertilizers, and nutrients according to research protocols, labels, University procedures, and applicable regulations. Follow and promote established safety procedures and University policies. Perform other related duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: (Required for Consideration at Either Level) High school diploma or GED and relevant agricultural operations, machinery, or related experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Knowledge of agricultural production practices and experience operating, adjusting, and maintaining agricultural machinery and equipment. Mechanical aptitude and ability to maintain and repair agricultural equipment, irrigation systems, vehicles, and related infrastructure. Ability to perform precise field operations and follow research protocols, field plans, application instructions, and equipment settings. Experience with, or ability to learn, precision agriculture technologies, computerized equipment controls, and electronic data collection systems. Ability to work independently on assigned tasks and adapt to changing weather, research, and operational conditions. Effective communication and interpersonal skills and the ability to work cooperatively with faculty, staff, students, researchers, and seasonal employees. Basic computer skills, including email, electronic recordkeeping, Word, and Excel. Class A Commercial Driver's License (CDL) and Delaware Pesticide Applicator and Nutrient Management Applicator certifications preferred at hire; required credentials must be obtained within established timeframes. ADDITIONAL QUALIFICATIONS BY LEVEL: Farm Assistant II - Grade 26N High school diploma or GED and three years of agricultural operations, machinery, or related experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience operating, adjusting, and maintaining agricultural machinery and equipment. Mechanical aptitude and ability to perform routine maintenance and repairs on agricultural equipment, irrigation systems, vehicles, and related infrastructure. Ability to follow instructions, work independently on assigned tasks, and perform work according to established procedures and protocols. Farm Assistant III - Grade 27N High school diploma or GED and five years of agricultural operations, machinery, or related experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Demonstrated experience operating, adjusting, calibrating, maintaining, and repairing agricultural machinery and equipment. Demonstrated mechanical aptitude and experience troubleshooting agricultural equipment, irrigation systems, vehicles, and related infrastructure. Ability to independently organize multiple priorities, exercise sound judgment, troubleshoot problems, and adapt to changing weather, research, and operational needs. Ability to perform precise research field operations while maintaining plot integrity and data quality. Ability to assist with planning, coordinating, and scheduling farm operations supporting multiple research projects and seasonal activities. Ability to provide operational guidance to seasonal employees, students, and other personnel as assigned. SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS: (Applicable to Both Levels) Must possess and maintain a valid driver's license and meet University vehicle operation requirements. Must obtain and maintain a Class A CDL within six months of employment and applicable Delaware Pesticide Applicator and Nutrient Management Applicator certifications within University-established timeframes. Must successfully complete required medical evaluations and remain qualified to use respiratory protection. Must routinely lift and carry up to 50 pounds and perform physically demanding agricultural and maintenance work. Must be able to work in confined spaces, climb ladders and work at heights exceeding 30 feet, and work above or below floor level. Must safely operate agricultural machinery, vehicles, power tools, irrigation systems, and related equipment. Must work indoors and outdoors in varying conditions, including heat, cold, rain, humidity, dust, and mud, with potential exposure to agricultural chemicals, fuels, noise, and other farm-related hazards. Must properly use required personal protective equipment (PPE), including respiratory protection when applicable. Must be available outside normal business hours when required for planting, irrigation, pesticide applications, harvest, weather events, equipment failures, and other time-sensitive operations. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503839/applications-programmer-iii-office-of-educational-technology": {
      "title": "Applications Programmer III, Office of Educational Technology",
      "description": "Applications Programmer III, Office of Educational Technology Apply now Job no: 503839 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Information Technology Pay Grade: 29S Context of Job: The Office of Educational Technology (OET) provides technology support to faculty and staff in the College of Education and Human Development (CEHD), to other departments within the University, and to clients external to the University of Delaware. As part of its services, OET provides web and database development and design and mobile app creation. The Applications Programmer III implements the application development life cycle. The incumbent leads all phases of the process including design, documentation, and development of custom applications for the web and desktop and mobile devices. The Applications Programmer III develops estimates and proposals for work to be completed by OET. The incumbent also communicates with clients, OET staff, UD Office of Communications and Marketing and IT staff, and third-party vendors, as needed, to provide quality custom software. This position reports to the Manager of the Office of Educational Technology. Major Responsibilities: • Manage application, web, and database development projects, including meeting with clients to determine requirements, engage in solution design (independently or with internal or external collaborators, as appropriate), and follow all appropriate project management procedures including UD’s technology request process when required. • Management and development of complex, responsive and robust WordPress themes requiring knowledge of the WordPress API, PHP, MySQL, and jQuery. • Manage the development of custom web applications written in C#/ASP.NET. • Manage the development of custom databases that may not use a web interface in Microsoft SQL, MySQL, and PostgreSQL. • Manage the use of generative AI in these and other applications as requested within the guidelines of UD policies for such technology as well as industry best practices. • Manage the work of student workers or other contract hires. • Ensure that websites, databases, web applications, and mobile applications comply with all UD data security, data privacy, accessibility and content policies, as well as industry best practices. • Research and implement best practices for developing code, including choosing programming platforms, productivity tools, and systems. • Maintain and update repositories of documentation shared by OET and its clients for all custom applications. • Maintain shared code repositories of custom applications that track code changes and make code accessible to OET staff. • Within a team and independently, develop and deliver presentations and special programs to CEHD students, faculty, and staff and external clients on WordPress, STEM-based classroom activities, and other topics. • Develop and manage delivery of educational applications for faculty and staff in compliance with all known government (i.e., Child Online Protection Act) and University of Delaware (data security and privacy policies, terms of use) policies. • Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications: • Bachelor’s degree with three years of relevant experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. An educational technology or teaching background is preferred. • Experience managing the work of others. • Excellent oral and written communication skills and organizational skills. • Detail oriented, able to manage multiple projects, and able to work within a team or independently. • Desire to learn new skills. • Two years of experience developing custom WordPress sites and themes, including using custom types, WordPress hooks, WordPress filters, and PHP, or four years of experience customizing another web content management system preferred. • Two years of experience developing custom web applications using ASP.NET and SQL Server, or equivalent programming language and database combination preferred. • Two years of experience developing mobile applications for iOS or Android mobile platforms preferred. • Two years of experience designing and delivering presentations to diverse groups, including technical and non-technical users preferred. • One year of experience programming or working with generative AI (such as Copilot, Claude, or ChatGPT) preferred. • Experience with web accessibility standards such as WCAG preferred. • Experience with the latest WordPress block-based editor preferred. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503840/academic-advisor-ii-college-of-earth-ocean-and-environment": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor II, College of Earth, Ocean, and Environment",
      "description": "Academic Advisor II, College of Earth, Ocean, and Environment Apply now Job no: 503840 College / VP Area: College of Earth & Ocean Envir Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support PAY GRADE: 29E Context of the Job: Under the general direction of the Senior Assistant Dean this position advises students regarding information, procedures and academic requirements of majors and minors in The College of Earth, Ocean, and Environment (CEOE). The advisor works cooperatively with the Senior Assistant Dean to set goals and propose solutions to advising issues that affect the quality of services to undergraduate students in CEOE. Major Responsibilities: Advise freshmen to sophomore students in all CEOE majors. Interpret policies, explain academic standing, review transfer credits and transcripts, and provide general guidance on academic matters including course scheduling, registration and academic /career options. Maintain advising assignments. Provide support and referral services to students, working with offices such as the Office of the Registrar, Disability Support Services, UD Careers, Academic Enrichment, and other auxiliary offices on campus that support student success. Make appropriate referrals to the Academic Program Manager as needed for additional support. Serve as a contact for prospective students calling the office or visiting for tours to inquire about available programs; answer questions accurately and completely and refers prospective students to appropriate faculty members as needed for further information. Maintain confidential medical documentation and issue excused absence notification on behalf of the Senior Assistant Dean. Assist with the processing of course substitutions through Stellic. Assist in coordination of on-campus college-level visits for prospective students alongside Associate Director of Recruitment and Events to support academic and prerequisite inquiries. Provide support for all aspects of CEOE New Student Orientation for undergraduate students, including NSO advising appointments and Meet Your College sessions. Attend and assist with major undergraduate events, both virtually and in person as needed, including Convocation, Honors Day, Major Exploration Week, Blue and Golden Saturdays, and Admitted Student Days. Correspond with students, faculty, and staff regarding academic deadlines throughout the academic year. Utilize Stellic to note advisement interactions, conduct targeted advising campaigns, and conduct proactive, targeted outreach to undergraduate students. Send reminder emails to students regarding important academic opportunities Oversee all report requests for undergraduate information; pull administrative reports. Advise students, faculty, and staff on the transfer credit evaluation process and procedures; refers students to webpage. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree with three years of related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience in academic advising in higher education. Ability to grasp, retain and disseminate an extensive, varied and dynamic body of information. Ability to handle highly sensitive activities with confidentiality, work independently and/or as part of a team, adapt to change and maintain flexibility. Working knowledge of college student counseling and advisement practices at the university level. Ability to evaluate student transcripts and/or records. Ability to communicate to students curricular and graduation requirements. Strong analytical and critical thinking skills. Ability to analyze, summarize and effectively present data. Effective leadership, organizational, interpersonal and oral and written communication skills. Ability to present information effectively to groups and to work productively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Proficiency with PowerPoint, Excel, and Word. Proficiency with a student information system. Special Requirements : Will be required to occasionally work evenings and weekends. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503843/academic-advisor-ii-hospitality-business-management": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor II, Hospitality Business Management",
      "description": "Academic Advisor II, Hospitality Business Management Apply now Job no: 503843 College / VP Area: Lerner College Business & Econ Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support GRADE: 29E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The University of Delaware’s business school, the Alfred Lerner College of Business and Economics, is a hub for innovation, business and economics research and career success. With hands-on experiences and a technology-driven curriculum, Lerner equips students to navigate the evolving business world and drive change worldwide. Under the general direction of the Department Chair of Hospitality & Sport Business Management (with input from the Senior Assistant Dean, Student Services), the Academic Advisor II is responsible for providing academic advising services to freshman through senior-level Hospitality Industry Management and Hospitality Business Management majors in the Department of Hospitality & Sport Business Management of the Lerner College of Business & Economics. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Advise undergraduate students, including dual enrollment, exchange and honors students, from matriculation through graduation, help with degree requirements, academic policies, registration, experiential learning, study abroad programs, internships, and graduation requirements. Support student success, retention, and degree completion by monitoring academic progress, implementing early intervention strategies, evaluating transfer coursework, conducting degree audits, guiding students through graduation, and connecting students with appropriate academic and well-being resources. Monitor key performance indicators related to student engagement, satisfaction, retention, graduation, and career outcomes. Provide data to the Department Chair for review. Support departmental evaluation and continuous improvement efforts. Represent the Hospitality department at Blue & Gold Days, Decision Days, Discovery Days, New Student Orientation, transfer events, and other University recruitment events to engage with prospective students and families to promote the Hospitality program. Participate in Lerner College undergraduate events including convocation, academic orientations, major fairs, and probation meetings. Handle the logistics of the Paul Wise speaker series. Perform senior checkouts to include reviewing student transcripts for completion of degree requirements, submission of course substitution requests, and communication with students regarding degree completion and accuracy of audits. Contacts students on probation or in academic difficulty through individual contact, and continual follow-up. Serve as the contact for prospective and transfer students. Advise students on applicability of transfer credits. Administer and support current and future dual enrollment agreements and other high-school related recruiting activities together with department chair and faculty. Inform students of the opportunities available within Lerner Career Services such as: professional development and career readiness programming, workshops, résumé reviews, LinkedIn training, mock interviews, professional etiquette programs, mentoring programs, employer information sessions, networking events, and industry panels. Act as a liaison between the Hospitality department and Lerner Career Services. Support Hospitality recruitment and enrollment initiatives through the collection of student success stories and the development of advising related communication materials, assist with the coordination of student ambassadors, collaborate with Admissions and Lerner College recruitment staff to support departmental strategies for enrollment growth. Provide administrative support for the engagement and activities of the Hospitality Advisory Board as well as alumni engagement efforts. Perform other job-related duties as assigned QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree and three years of related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Degree in student personnel, college counseling or other related field preferred. Master’s degree preferred. A strong customer service orientation Knowledge of hospitality, and/or sport business education preferred, including neighboring fields like travel and tourism management or real estate. Effective presentation and group facilitation skills. Ability to present effectively to small and large groups and to work productively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Ability to grasp, retain, and disseminate an extensive and varied body of information. Ability to exercise sound judgment and employ decision making skills under the guidance of the supervisor. Ability to evaluate student transcripts and/or records. Knowledge of academic standards governing student probation, suspension, and/or expulsion and student recruitment and retention issues. Ability to communicate student course prerequisites, certification, and/or curriculum/graduation requirements. Advanced working knowledge of advisement policies, procedures, systems, regulations, and standards at the university level. Effective analytical and critical thinking skills. Ability to analyze, summarize, and effectively present data. Proficient computer skills including use of presentation software, Excel, Zoom, and experience with UDSIS. Ability to communicate professionally and employ tact and diplomacy with multiple stakeholders including students, parents, faculty, alumni, corporate representatives, other universities, government officials and funding agencies. Special requirements : Ability to work a flexible schedule, as the responsibilities of the position will require participation in evening and weekend activities. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
