    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503959/tenuretrack-faculty-position-in-biomedical-engineering-musculoskeletal-bioengineering": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Biomedical Engineering Musculoskeletal Bioengineering",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Biomedical Engineering Musculoskeletal Bioengineering Apply now Job no: 503959 College / VP Area: College of Engineering Work type: Faculty Location: Newark, DE Categories: Biomedical Engineering Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Biomedical Engineering Musculoskeletal Bioengineering The Biomedical Engineering Department (BME, www.bme.udel.edu ) at the University of Delaware (UD) invites applications for tenure-track assistant professor in basic and preclinical Musculoskeletal Research that includes, but is not limited to, understanding the physical and biological mechanisms of musculoskeletal structure and function, applied research in musculoskeletal disorders, and strategies for therapeutic interventions. Research can span a range of length scales from organisms to molecules. Successful applicants will be part of the Delaware Center for Musculoskeletal Research ( DCMR ), an NIH-funded Center for Biomedical Research Excellence (COBRE). The DCMR recently received NIH Phase II funding to support multiple new faculty across collaborating departments with opportunities for joint appointments. The DCMR provides research infrastructure and supports faculty success with mentoring and funding opportunities. UD BME is ABET accredited with world class research programs in musculoskeletal biomechanics, disease modeling, drug delivery, nanomedicine, neuro-engineering, and cell and tissue engineering. Successful applicants will share in our vision to be a BME department recognized for high impact research and delivering an exceptional engineering education. We seek creative individuals eager to work in a collaborative interdisciplinary environment, have demonstrated research excellence, are outstanding educators and mentors, and will contribute to a climate that attracts and affirms students from various backgrounds. UD BME has new state-of-the-art research laboratories located on the STAR (Science, Technology, and Advanced Research) Campus, a hub of biomedical research and innovation at UD. Core facilities across the UD campus include: the DCMR Research Core (pre-clinical models, in vivo animal imaging, histology, and biomechanics), the Center for Biomedical and Brain Imaging (3T human and 9.4T small animal/specimen MRIs), the Delaware Biotechnology Institute (world-class Bio-Imaging Center, DNA sequencing & genotyping, bioinformatics, and proteomics cores), and core labs for human-based studies. Access to active outpatient clinics is fostered through long-standing partnerships with UD’s outstanding Physical Therapy Department. UD BME also enjoys close relationships and opportunities for innovative clinical and translational collaborations with Nemours Children ’s Health, Thomas Jefferson University , the University of Pennsylvania, and others. UD is home to several outstanding institutes, including the Institute for Engineering Driven Health, the Data Science Institute, and the National Institute of Innovation in Manufacturing Biopharmaceuticals (NIIMBL). UD’s Newark, Delaware campus provides a vibrant college town with convenient access to the major cities of the East Coast. Candidates must have a Ph.D. degree in engineering or a related field. For full consideration, applicants should submit: a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement (max 4 pages), a statement on teaching and mentoring (max 2 pages), a statement on leadership and service (max 2 pages), and contact information for at least 3 references. For priority consideration, applications must be received by Dec 15, 2026 , although the search will remain open until the position is filled. The University of Delaware values inclusive excellence and is committed to fostering a community where all students, faculty, and staff can thrive. Persons from all backgrounds are encouraged to apply. The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. For information, please contact the Search Committee Chair Dawn Elliott ( delliott@udel.edu ). Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503953/post-doctoral-researcher-department-of-geography-and-spatial-sciences": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher, Department of Geography and Spatial Sciences",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Researcher, Department of Geography and Spatial Sciences Apply now Job no: 503953 College / VP Area: College of Earth & Ocean Envir Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory Pay Grade: 90E Project Summary The Department of Geography and Spatial Sciences at the University of Delaware is seeking a Postdoctoral Researcher to characterize current and future U.S. severe convective storm (SCS) risk for insurance applications. The project goals are to (1) estimate present-day and future SCS risk using simulations from large ensemble systems and (2) identify and quantify the uncertainty in historical trends and near-term model projections. The work will be in close collaboration with scientists at Columbia University and NOAA Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Lab as well as industry professionals across the (re)insurance sector to develop SCS risk assessments and decision-making tools/frameworks using near-term SCS projections. Focus Area: Severe Weather, Climate Risk Background Severe convective storms (SCS) — thunderstorms that produce damaging winds, large hail, or tornadoes — are increasingly becoming a dominant driver of insured losses. Consequently, assessments of present-day as well as projected future SCS risk are of considerable interest and value to the insurance industry. Our group will use historical and future simulations in large ensembles to better resolve risk from SCS hazards like tornadoes and hail, and to tailor risk assessments and near-term SCS projections to meet the needs of the insurance industry. Responsibility Develop data-driven models to simulate severe weather hazards like tornadoes and hail, and apply models to investigate severe weather hazard activity in large ensemble simulations. Evaluate model biases, e.g. from GFDL’s Seamless System for Prediction and EArth System Research (SPEAR) large ensembles. Identify and explore sources of uncertainty in near-term SCS projections, e.g., from interannual to decadal climate variability. Publish findings on peer-reviewed journals, present progress at professional meetings, prepare project reports and deliverables, and collaborate with other research and industry teams on a regular basis. Qualifications Ph.D. in a relevant field (e.g., Atmospheric Science, Meteorology, Climate Science, Geography, or related areas) or should be completed with their Ph.D. degree requirements by January 2027. Experience with weather and/or climate model output (e.g., SPEAR, WRF, CESM), and familiarity with climate model frameworks (e.g., large ensembles). Proficiency in programming and data analysis (e.g., Python) and handling large datasets, such as from hindcasts, large ensembles, or Coupled Model Intercomparison Project. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently as well as effectively in a collaborative, multidisciplinary team environment. Preferred Skills Experience with data-driven (including machine learning), physics-informed modeling approaches. Background in climate risk, i.e., applying weather and climate to societal impacts, decision-making, adaptation, etc. Familiarity with high-performance computing. This position is in-person with support for conference travel and other professional development activities. To Apply Any questions can be directed to Dr. Kelsey Malloy; kmmalloy@udel.edu Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503979/administrative-specialist-lerner-student-services": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist, Lerner Student Services",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist, Lerner Student Services Apply now Job no: 503979 College / VP Area: Lerner College Business & Econ Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support GRADE: 29E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The University of Delaware’s business school, the Alfred Lerner College of Business and Economics, is a hub for innovation, business and economics research and career success. With hands-on experiences and a technology-driven curriculum, Lerner equips students to navigate the evolving business world and drive change worldwide. Under the general supervision of the Senior Assistant Dean and the Director of Lerner Career Services, the Administrative Specialist oversees the administrative and fiscal operations supporting Lerner Undergraduate Advising, Lerner Career Services, and undergraduate recruitment activities. The Administrative Specialist monitors departmental budgets and expenditures; coordinates administrative processes, events, and front-desk operations; prepares enrollment and other administrative reports; manages departmental data and reporting systems; supervises support staff and student employees; coordinates web and communications activities; and serves as a liaison between the unit and the Lerner College Business Office and other University offices. The position is responsible for applying and interpreting University policies and procedures, assisting with policy development, analyzing administrative practices, and recommending improvements to enhance operational effectiveness and service delivery. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Manage the day-to-day administrative and operational functions of the Lerner Undergraduate Advising and Lerner Career Services units and support undergraduate recruitment activities. Process requisitions, invoices, purchasing transactions, and other financial documents, including preparing and administering departmental purpose codes and budgets. Administer departmental purchasing card transactions. Monitor and review departmental expenditures for appropriate funding, reporting, and policy compliance. Ensure the accuracy and availability of funding and compliance with standard accounting and audit procedures. Ensure the smooth and effective operation of the unit’s administrative office and front-desk reception services. Assist the Senior Assistant Dean with confidential correspondence and sensitive student matters, including issues related to medical leaves, dismissals, and suspensions. Handle confidential information in accordance with university policies and applicable privacy requirements. Supervise non-exempt support staff, miscellaneous workers, graduate students, and student employees; hire, motivate, develop, direct, and assign work; monitor workload and productivity; evaluate performance; provide staff training and development; provide assistance in analyzing and resolving complex issues. Plan, coordinate, and conduct projects requiring judgment in the independent evaluation, selection, and substantial adaptation and modification of standard techniques. Work closely with the College events management team to plan and coordinate major undergraduate events. Coordinate First-Year Student Welcome Back, Honors Day, and undergraduate Convocation ceremonies. Assist with program production, logistics, and event-related communications. Provide occasional weekend support for Convocation, recruiting events, and other major University activities. Work with Lerner Marketing/Communication team to support mass communications to students and prospective students. Provide information, resolve problems, complete surveys, and prepare reports in support of unit operations. Implement administrative policies and procedures established by the Senior Assistant Dean and Director and recommend revisions as needed. Ensure compliance with university policies and procedures. Order, maintain, and inventory office supplies and equipment. Provide a high level of customer service to students, faculty, staff, alumni, prospective students and families, and employers both in person and by telephone. Maintain exempt and non-exempt vacation and sick leave records and timesheets for student workers and miscellaneous wage employees. Review and process student requests for documented excused absences. Manage job postings, create job offers, and oversee the complete onboarding process for student workers and miscellaneous wage employees through TalentLink. Ensure timely completion of employment documentation, system access, and applicable regulatory and University requirements. Supervise non-exempt support staff, miscellaneous wage employees, graduate students, and student employees. Design and generate reports using Tableau, and Stellic. Manipulate, and maintain spreadsheets and databases to support departmental and College reporting needs. Assign academic advisors in UDSIS and maintain accurate student and advising data. Manage the departmental scholarship application process, including collecting, organizing, and maintaining application information. Process scholarship payments and related documentation. Assist in administering the Lerner Professional Attire Fund. Support the Associate Director for Undergraduate Recruiting in preparing for undergraduate recruitment events. Assist with the preparation and ordering of print materials, supplies, promotional items, and other recruitment materials. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Master’s degree preferred. Experience in budget tracking, fiscal management, and financial reconciliation. Supervisory experience, including the ability to assign work, monitor performance, and support employee development. Strong organizational, analytical, problem-solving, and time-management skills. Demonstrated ability to work independently and exercise sound judgment in handling complex administrative matters. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated ability to provide a high level of customer service to individuals of varying ages, backgrounds, and roles. Events management experience preferred. Advanced proficiency with word processing, spreadsheet, database, web authoring and/or presentation software. Experience working with data, spreadsheets, reporting systems, and administrative databases. Knowledge of TalentLink, Tableau, UDSIS and Stellic preferred. Ability to interpret and apply policies and procedures and communicate requirements clearly to others. SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS: Occasional evening and weekend work required, particularly in support of Convocation, undergraduate recruitment events, and other major College or University programs. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504009/director-communications-college-of-arts-and-sciences": {
      "title": "Director, Communications, College of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Director, Communications, College of Arts and Sciences Apply now Job no: 504009 College / VP Area: Communication & Marketing Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support, Communications & Public Relations Pay Grade: 32E Context of the Job: The Communications Director plays a key role in developing and crafting the internal and external messaging of the University of Delaware’s College of Arts and Sciences (CAS), in conjunction with UD’s Office of Communications and Marketing (OCM). Reporting to and under the general direction of the Sr. Director, College Communications in OCM with a dotted line to the Dean of the College, the Communications Director provides leadership and develops the strategic communications and marketing plan for CAS. The Communications Director works directly with colleagues in OCM to provide public relations, writing, graphic design and print production, digital and multimedia production, event planning and photography services for CAS and its respective units. The Communications Director is responsible for projecting a favorable image of the college to its stakeholders and the media, and for advocating on behalf of the college and the industries/agencies it supports. Specifically, the Communications Director is responsible for producing communications pieces and strategies for both internal and external audiences to address the strategic goals and objectives of CAS and its units, as well as the overarching goals and objectives of the University. Major Responsibilities: Develops and implements strategic communications plans and initiatives for CAS in support of college and University priorities and goals. Leads the development and implementation of a comprehensive digital, social and multi-media strategy that increases CAS engagement with key audiences and is aligned with UD’s digital and multimedia media policy and complies with ADA requirements. Works closely with communications professionals to coordinate production and delivery of communications tactics and products, ensuring adherence to strategic goals, policies and procedures. Interacts with units internal to the college to address strategic communications goals, including but not limited to the CAS Undergraduate Academic Services office for communication with prospective students and their parents, the college’s academic departments and centers. Interacts regularly with UD groups outside of CAS, including but not limited to communications units in other UD colleges, the Office of Development and Alumni Relations, Admissions, and external media entities to ensure that information on CAS programs is communicated effectively and favorably to internal and external audiences at local, regional, national and international levels. Develops targeted marketing and communications messaging for the college across multiple channels including print, web and social media. Provides communications guidance and support for major CAS events and CAS participation in major university events. Creates dynamic written and digital content for multiple diverse audiences, including current and potential students and parents (recruitment), alumni, internal and external stakeholders and various publics. Advises senior CAS administrators on the effective communication of goals, priorities and actions to stakeholders and media. Serves as the college’s point person for strategic communications, particularly in emergent situations or major developments that receive media or stakeholder attention. Perform miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in journalism, communications, marketing or related discipline, and six years of related experience managing and delivering communications activities, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience in a research-oriented institution of higher education preferred. Ability to work effectively, manage multiple clients and projects simultaneously and under tight deadlines, often switching gears multiple times a day among a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community, while managing competing priorities and overseeing the day-to-day effort of supporting professional staff, partners and/or vendors. Superior written, verbal, interpersonal, organizational and public speaking skills, and attention to detail. Ability to apply knowledge, best practices, policies and procedures to specific communications and marketing plans and activities. Knowledge of communications concepts, principles, methodology and techniques. Knowledge and understanding of current issues and trends applicable to higher education institutions. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503978/director-business-operations": {
      "title": "Director, Business Operations",
      "description": "Director, Business Operations Apply now Job no: 503978 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support, Health Services PAY GRADE: 32E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: Under limited direction of the Sr. Business Officer, the Director of Business Operations is responsible for the fiscal management and cross-organizational business operations in the college including implementation of University-wide central systems, processes and policies as well as oversight of college initiatives and strategies to improve business practice and organizational efficiency. Through financial/budgetary projections and analyses of actual activity, this position leads departments with strategy development and decision-making. This position serves as a key role in providing information on college-wide activity including strengths and challenges to the Sr. Business Officer and Dean to facilitate decision making and planning. A high level of independent judgment, initiative and problem-solving skills, and the ability to demonstrate a high degree of confidentiality and discretion are required. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Strategic Operations Lead College strategic (long-term) and tactical (daily) efforts through support of the department/unit business operations including the provision of analytics and process expertise on a wide range of complex issues/problems surrounding the college enrollment, cost efficiencies, and financial and business systems. Design and implement improved methods for managing work across the entire college, establish cross-training and career paths within college administrative functions. Provide individual training for College and departmental users in all aspects of business operations and financial monitoring and reporting following UD guidelines. Serve as the CHS business approver for financial, human resources, and administrative transactions/Webforms, ensuring compliance with University policies, budgetary requirements, and applicable regulations. Review and approve personnel actions, salary and compensation changes, supplemental payments, purchasing and procurement transactions, reimbursements, and other business operations processes. Exercise independent judgment in evaluating requests, maintaining appropriate internal controls, and ensuring the accurate stewardship of institutional resources. Responsible for the development of material and creation/maintenance of the College Staff Resource webpage and MS Teams Channel (training documents, calendar of deliverables, policies documents, etc.) Participate in the development of college vision and strategic planning activities. Budget and Planning Assist in the reporting and monitoring of the College and Departmental budget and expenses throughout-year to create financial projections, updates college leadership, and create ad-hoc analysis (i.e., business plans, metrics reporting, etc.) that inform business needs, develops sound justifications, and aids in prioritization framed by university/college/department goals Oversees the approval and processing of revenue, expenditure and department budgets, budget turnaround updates, and the maintenance of accounts and ledgers, ensuring compliance with appropriate government/University regulations and policies, and ensuring maintenance of appropriate internal controls safeguards Works closely with Sr. Business Officer to prepare and complete fiscal year year-end close and financial performance for college, departments and self-supporting units. Works closely with the Sr. Business Officer and the Dean to set department operating budgets, undergraduate and graduate funding, temporary teaching funds and student and faculty recruiting budgets. Serves as College budget and planning authority by providing guidance to Department Business Administrators and Administrative Managers for the interpretation of financial reports and data, College and University policies, and in managing costs and leveraging resources to fulfill strategic goals. Provide strategic consultation and independently perform complex financial analyses, including revenue and expense projections, and analyses of actual activity and translate this for department/unit planning and needs assessment and to Sr. Business Office, Dean and college; leadership serve as the expert on department business as it influences goals and financials. Procurement Management Serve as the Procurement Manager for the college responsible for overseeing the procurement process, including credit card purchases, accounts payable, and accounts receivable. Responsible for college-wide implementation and training for Concur and UD Exchange. Responsible for implementation at the College level of all new Procurement Policy/Process and updates to ensure the college remains in compliance. Supervisory Direct supervisor for the Dean’s Office Business Administrator, who is responsible for managing and overseeing financial operational activities for the Dean’s Office and its functional units. Dotted-line supervision to the seven department Business Administrator I and II’s who are responsible for the managing the operations within their respective college department. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree and six years related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Degree in Accounting, Finance, or Business-related field preferred. Ability to demonstrate a high level of independent judgment, initiative and problem-solving skills. Ability to demonstrate a high degree of confidentiality and discretion. Proficient knowledge in budget preparation, analysis and financial management. Experience in a higher education institution preferred. Strong attention to detail, analytical, critical thinking and decision-making skills. Advanced proficiency with spreadsheet, database, word processing, and presentation software. Experience with UD financial system with report generation skills highly preferred. Ability to be flexible, work independently and have high accountability for a large volume of work. Ability to handle multiple assignments concurrently. Ability to analyze and interpret data and make independent decisions. Effective communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to interact with people of diverse backgrounds, skills and abilities. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
