    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503985/administrative-assistant-iii-college-of-education-human-development": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant III, College of Education & Human Development",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant III, College of Education & Human Development Apply now Job no: 503985 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support PAY GRADE: 27N CONTEXT OF THE JOB : Under limited supervision of the Director of CEETP, the Administrative Assistant III manages and supports background clearances for all teacher preparation students across 26 programs, university-wide clearances, and special clearances for child care licensing. Performs complex administrative duties including hiring and managing work study students, manages financial payments for K-12 partners and university-based field personnel, maintains the OCS website, communicates with school partners regarding clearances, surveys and evaluations, and manages related data using ACCESS, TaskStream, Google Suite, UDSIS (UD Student Information System), and Student Health records. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Receive, process, and monitor three background clearances: Child Protection Registry, DE state & federal criminal background, health screenings (TB/PPD tests) and Youth Protection training for all education preparation undergraduate, graduate students (1,500+/semester), and non-education students that have a field experience with minors. Inform UD faculty of students' eligibility for all field placements (miscellaneous field work, early fields, methods, and student teaching/internships) as determined by the outcome of background clearances. Manage all financial/compensation aspects of payment and distribution of clock hours, process honoraria/stipends for clinical educators, site supervisors, work study students, and gas reimbursement for field instructors. Manage all aspects of work related to clinical educators' engagement with teacher preparation programs including informing teachers about placement procedures and confirmations via semester-based placement letters, provide support to help teachers navigate university-data collection systems (TaskStream) in order to enable timely completion of surveys and evaluations. Interview, hire and supervise work study students (data entry, spreadsheet management, filing of confidential information, and shredding). Ensure submission of work study student timesheets in UD Workforce. Maintain Office of Clinical Studies information by updating the OCS website that may include uploading new forms, revising and updating protocols, policies, calendars, and other pertinent information. Access, input, extract, and respond to data report requests using the following databases or database management systems: Access, TaskStream, Google Suite, Student Health Records, UDSIS. Monitor the distribution of surveys/evaluation forms including Title 1 reports, candidates’ program evaluation, and other surveys related to methods courses. Communicate and cooperate with Delaware state, intra-state agencies, and relevant contacts outside of the University-based system including Department of Services for Children Youth and Families, DE State Police, School District Personnel, Office of Child Care Licensing, Delaware CHASE Agency, and transportation vendors. Handle all correspondence for the Office of Clinical Studies (email, phone, in person). Provide quality written and oral communication with the College of Education and Human Development (CEHD) Dean’s office, Student Health staff, faculty & staff across departments, and Delaware State and Intra-State agencies. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: High School Diploma or GED and three years of experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Associate’s degree preferred. Higher education experience preferred. Extensive knowledge of the principles, protocols, and practices of office procedures and management. Proficient in utilizing spreadsheets and database software including Access, PowerPoint, WordPress, Mail Merge, Microsoft and Google Suites. Ability to learn new technology including programs, platforms, reporting systems, student information systems. Ability to analyze and interpret data to create reports for various entities. Ability to multitask and work under limited supervision. Ability to exercise discretion on confidential matters. Effective communication skills and the ability to interact professionally with students, parents, faculty, district partners, clinical educators/teachers, DE State Police, vendors. Maintains attention to detail and commitment to accuracy. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503986/grants-analyst-i-college-of-agriculture-and-natural-resources": {
      "title": "Grants Analyst I, College of Agriculture and Natural Resources",
      "description": "Grants Analyst I, College of Agriculture and Natural Resources Apply now Job no: 503986 College / VP Area: College of Ag & Natural Res Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Grants & Contracts Pay Grade: 29E Context of Job: Under the general direction of the Manager, Grants Administration, the Grants Analyst I manages the pre-award and some assigned post-award activities for a variety of faculty and with an assortment of Federal and State agencies, foundations, and private industry. Faculty support includes assisting the PI/Project Directors in developing competitive grant proposals, assisting in preparation of project budgets, and interpret and ensure that submission guidelines are followed. The Grants Analyst should have a clear understanding of federal, state and university policies and procedures with regards to research administration. The Grants Analyst I also maintains productive relationships with constituencies related to the contracts and grants operation in the College. The Grants Analyst I is part of the contracts and grants team in the College of Agriculture and Natural Resources (CANR). Sponsored program expenditures at CANR is approximately $23M annually. This position is responsible for ensuring accurate and efficient proposal submission of contracts and grants from Federal, State, and industrial sponsors, working with faculty and researchers within the four Academic departments and Cooperative Extension within the College. Major Responsibilities: Provide direct support of pre-award tasks including close communication with the PI. Review the solicitation and sponsor requirements to guide the process including observation of potentially difficult guidelines, match requirements, etc.; recommend budget, certifications, effort committed and matching funds requests. Create, collect and review complex proposal information including budget, budget justification, and proposal documents. Communicate with subrecipient and collaborator participants to ensure completion of subrecipient proposal materials including budget, statement of work and other sponsor and Research Office required documents. Act as liaison between faculty member and Research Office on all proposal matters. In collaboration with the PI, develop competitive proposals in support of securing independent funding for projects. Recommend matching funds sources and advise PI on optimal use of resources. Ensure proper submission of proposal information into PeopleSoft Grants, Cayuse, Research.gov and other sponsor sites as required. Verify accuracy of proposal information prior to submission to Research Office for approval. Assist the Manager, Grants Administration with Delegated Authority proposals and awards. Assist the Grants Manager with subaward processing and management. Perform miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Accounting, Finance or other related field with two years related experience, or equivalent combination of education and/or experience. Skill in budget preparation for contracts and grants – pre-award administration. Knowledge of 2 CFR 200 Uniform Guidance and other pertinent external regulations required with ability to interpret, adapt and apply guidelines and procedures. Ability to use advanced techniques in spreadsheet and database management. Experience with grants.gov and various sponsor databases. Ability to analyze budgetary plans for compliance with approved policies and procedures. Excellent interpersonal skills and patience. Ability to work independently, strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to interpret, adapt and apply guidelines and procedures. Ability to communicate effectively with a wide range of constituents on a variety of research management and financial topics. Ability to interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds. Special Requirements: Must participate in training sponsored by the University Research Office. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503993/erp-cloud-system-lead-i": {
      "title": "ERP, Cloud System Lead I",
      "description": "ERP, Cloud System Lead I Apply now Job no: 503993 College / VP Area: Vice President for IT Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Information Technology Acts as the lead systems specialist in the Department of Enterprise Systems and Cloud Services. Leads the implementation of software development life cycle methodologies to enhance the University’s PeopleSoft Financial system and provides primary support for multiple PeopleSoft modules through planning, requirements analysis, design, development, integration, testing, implementation, maintenance, and documentation in accordance with IT-MIS policies and standards. Duties include: manage multiple projects and support multiple critical applications; assist junior Applications Programmers with solutions to complex problems; maintain, tests, and update PeopleSoft ERP systems; improve performance of PeopleSoft batch programs, reports and online pages using techniques such as SQL tuning, database indexes, hints, statistics and explain plans; plan, upgrade, and deploy new PeopleTools versions and PeopleSoft application versions using PeopleSoft upgrade assistant, fit-gap analysis, data conversion, system and database audits, performance & integration testing; test and correct programs to ensure they work properly and to determine that programs meet client needs; develop statistical analysis and data analysis using PeopleSoft Query/XML Publisher reporting tools; coordinate work to develop, test, deploy and document PeopleSoft custom or delivered batch programs to interface with internal systems and to meet the University’s requirements; provide production support for the Financial system and maintains the daily production schedule of PeopleSoft systems; analyze and resolve production incidents; works with users and production control staff regarding schedule and problem resolution; provide on-call support for PeopleSoft systems; work closely with management to ensure adherence to UDIT policies and with users to determine their needs, recommend solutions, and keep them updated on project status; re-engineer business functions by evaluating requests for service and reviews policies, procedures, and workflows to ensure that the best systems are implemented; troubleshoot complex data processing problems and user data entry errors and coordinates problem resolution and testing with other programmers, users, and units; and participate (and may lead) in internal and external training programs. Requires a Bachelor’s degree or foreign equivalent degree in Information Systems, Computer Science, Engineering, Business, or a related field and 5 years of experience with Oracle ERP and IDE (i.e., PeopleSoft Financials, PeopleTools core technologies, PeopleSoft reporting technologies, PeopleSoft Upgrade/Change Assistant, Oracle Database, Oracle Procurement), programming languages, and database querying and software implementation techniques. Limited telework permitted – must be on-site two days per week Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Nov 15 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
