    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504003/part-time-research-technician-geomicrobiology-lab": {
      "title": "Part Time Research Technician, Geomicrobiology Lab",
      "description": "Part Time Research Technician, Geomicrobiology Lab Apply now Job no: 504003 College / VP Area: College of Earth & Ocean Envir Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory Position Title/ Assignment Name: Part-time research technician Anticipated Start Date: 01/04/2027 Hourly Pay Rate: $15-23.50 depending on qualifications and experience Anticipated Schedule: 4-5 days/week, 4-5 hours per day Anticipated Hours/Week: 20 Location: Ammon-Pinizzotto Biopharmaceutical Innovation Center at 590 Avenue 1743 Newark DE 19713 Role Description: The Chan Geomicrobiology Lab within the Department of Earth Sciences, University of Delaware, is looking for a part-time research technician to support funded research projects. The ideal candidate should be capable of aseptic technique, preparing and conducting laboratory experiments involving microorganisms, following written protocols, data collection and analysis, and clearly documenting results in written reports. Primary Responsibilities: Under the guidance of the Principal Investigator and an Associate Scientist assist with on-going experiments including media preparation, microbial culture maintenance, cell counting, microscopy, protein purification, proteomic sample preparation, and maintaining a safe, clean, and sterile work environment. Qualifications: (grade level, academic, major, courses taken, previous experience, etc.) Preference given to candidates with B.S. or B.A. in microbiology (preferred) or biology, biochemistry, molecular biology or related field, or relevant coursework (with labs), and/or prior research experience in microbiology or a related field. Hiring Timeline: Immediate start Application Review: We will start reviewing applications Nov. 1, 2026. Position will remain open until filled. Interviews-Date Range: Starting Nov. 8 until filled Offers Extended-Date Range : Starting Dec. 1 until filled Please note that dates are subject to change depending on the needs of the department. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Nov 30 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504053/financial-analyst-ii-office-of-the-provost": {
      "title": "Financial Analyst II, Office of the Provost",
      "description": "Financial Analyst II, Office of the Provost Apply now Job no: 504053 College / VP Area: Office of the Provost Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Financial Services and Procurement PAY GRADE: 31E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: Working under the direction of the Vice Provost for Academic Programs and University Initiatives, the Financial Analyst II is responsible for the financial operations of the large and complex university initiatives portfolio. The APUI portfolio includes the FinTech Innovation Hub, the Center for Innovation in Lifelong Learning and Teaching – which includes four units(Academic Technology Services, Center for Teaching Assessment and Learning, UD Online, and Professional and Continuing Studies (which includes the access center, non-credit programs, and Osher Lifelong Learning Institute)), and the Division of Enrollment Management. The Financial Analyst II interacts with and serves as liaison for the units in the portfolio, and coordinates with the business administrator in enrollment management. The person in this role will work closely with the budget office to ensure the financial health of the portfolio units and align the portfolio with university level goals and mission. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Assist and advise the Vice Provost for Academic Programs and University Initiatives on fiscal, space, and procurement matters. Manage the daily financial operations of units in the portfolio, with the exception of Enrollment Management. Coordinate with the business administrator in the Division of Enrollment Management to ensure the financial operations of the unit are in line with broader portfolio goals and financial objectives. Prepare and administer, in consultation with the Vice Provost, accounts and budgetary matters related to appointments and salary. Monitor expenditures, perform analyses, provide projections, and process requisitions. Oversee the budgets of all units in the portfolio, including basic budgets, endowments, discretionary funds, etc. Prepare projections for the portfolio and evaluates various planning scenarios as well as prepare and interpret financial reports for the Vice Provost. Develop financial models and develop forecasts to interpret financial projections for the portfolio Manage annual BTA and year-end budget reconciliations. Responsibility for obligations and expenditures for the budget and prepares monthly reports, analyses and projections about revenue and estimated expenditure, with recommendations for modifications. Participate in audits of portfolio accounts. Actively manage the portfolio and its units’ budgets. Participate in the budgeting and implementation of special initiatives. Provide accurate financial information to the Vice Provost and the reporting units in the portfolio. Prepare and present materials to summarize and justify unit/program budget requests, including data gathering/reporting and trend analyses in connection with budgetary formulation and administration. Ensure accurate, compliant and efficient management of the portfolio procurement activities, including Concur and UD Exchange workflow processes and transactions. Ensure proper implementation of policies and procedures in the areas of finance and operations to ensure adherence to Federal and University policies. Interpret and provide guidance regarding University policies and procedures for the Vice Provost. Provide orientation and interpretation of University financial policies and procedures for new portfolio unit leadership. Partner with the portfolio leadership to implement strategic operational objectives, and new program initiatives. Regularly meet with the Vice Provost and portfolio leadership to make recommendations regarding new administrative financial policies and procedures or changes and improvements to existing processes; develop and/or initiate new office forms and procedures. Manage fiscal affairs of the department, including budget creation/management planning, expenditure and costs analysis. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in accounting, finance, business administration, or a related field, and five years of job-related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience with budget preparation and management, financial reporting and analysis, forecasting, and financial planning. Experience using management systems, relational databases, spreadsheets and word-processing software programs. Familiarity with UD accounting system, PeopleSoft financials, and UD policies and procedures is preferred. Strong analytical, organizational, problem-solving, and written and verbal communication skills. Ability to interpret, adapt and apply guidelines and procedures. Ability to gather and analyze statistical and financial data and generate reports. Ability to develop, plan, and implement new strategies and procedures, and be part of continuous improvement. Ability to present information effectively to all levels of management. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503969/continuing-track-certified-nurse-midwife-faculty-school-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Continuing Track Certified Nurse Midwife Faculty, School of Nursing",
      "description": "Continuing Track Certified Nurse Midwife Faculty, School of Nursing Apply now Job no: 503969 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Faculty Location: Newark, DE Categories: Nursing School of Nursing Continuing Track Certified Nurse Midwife Faculty Positions (Appointment effective January 1, 2027 & August 16, 2027) DESCRIPTION: The School of Nursing in the College of Health Sciences at the University of Delaware seeks two experienced, innovative, and highly motivated candidates for 9-month, continuing track nurse midwife faculty positions. Successful candidates will join a faculty that includes nationally recognized leaders in clinical practice, education, research, and policy. In addition, the desired clinical area of expertise includes current national AMCB Board Certification as a Certified Nurse-Midwife (CNM) and current unrestricted state licensure. Expectations also include participation in scholarship, clinical practice and service activities. Faculty are encouraged to teach in both undergraduate and graduate nursing programs and contribute to a welcoming campus environment committed to fostering a positive workplace culture of belonging. The school of nursing is seeking two qualified candidates, one CNM at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin January 2027 and the second CNM Faculty at the rank of instructor or assistant professor faculty member to begin on August 2027. Assistant Professor — Anticipated start date: January 2027 This position requires appointment at the Assistant Professor rank. Required qualifications include: An earned DNP or PhD U.S. RN licensure and eligibility for Delaware RN licensure Certified Nurse-Midwife (CNM) certification and eligibility for Delaware CNM license APRN licensure or state equivalent At least five years of clinical practice experience as a CNM A record of successful teaching experience in graduate nursing programs, including online instruction Instructor or Assistant Professor — Anticipated start date: August 2027 This position may be filled at either the Instructor or Assistant Professor rank. Required qualifications include: A master’s degree for appointment at the instructor rank; an earned DNP or PhD is required for appointment at the Assistant Professor rank U.S. RN licensure and eligibility for Delaware RN licensure Certified Nurse-Midwife (CNM) certification and eligibility for Delaware CNM license APRN licensure or state equivalent At least five years of clinical practice experience as a CNM A record of successful teaching experience in graduate nursing programs, including online instruction, is preferred for this position For both positions, appointments at the applicable academic rank is subject to the candidate’s qualifications, experience, and departmental faculty vote, in accordance with the University’s collective bargaining agreement (CBA). GENERAL INFORMATION: The School of Nursing ( http://www.udel.edu/nursing/ ) is a thriving academic unit of over 32 faculty, approximately 775 undergraduate students, and 220 graduate students. Recognized as a Center of Excellence by the National League of Nursing and accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education, the School offers innovative, dynamic pre-licensure baccalaureate, master’s, post-master’s and post-baccalaureate doctoral programs, including the DNP and a PhD in Nursing Science, spanning face-to-face, hybrid, and fully online programs. The School of Nursing has a strong drive to continue growing its research program in areas of aging, symptom science, and health services research. The School of Nursing emphasizes interdisciplinary and translational research, interpersonal education, and service to the community in an environment that supports an environment committed to fostering a positive workplace culture of belonging. The Master of Science and Post-Master Certificate Nurse-Midwife programs are the first in the state of Delaware welcoming their inaugural cohort of students this Fall of 2026. The College of Health Sciences is a dynamic, innovative academic unit with 3,400 undergraduate and graduate students and 135 faculty, who are nationally recognized leaders across multiple disciplines. The College is housed in multiple buildings on the traditional main campus but also is an anchor tenant of the University’s Science, Technology, and Advanced Research ( STAR ) Campus. STAR has become an exciting center that combines business, research, education and simulation space, all focused on leading strategic priorities in health science, biopharmaceuticals, data science, engineering, finance and other cross-cutting fields of study. In addition to the Department of the School of Nursing, CHS has six additional academic units, including Communication Sciences and Disorders (#33 U.S. News Best Graduate Schools), Epidemiology, Health Behavior and Nutrition Sciences, Kinesiology and Applied Physiology, Medical and Molecular Sciences, and Physical Therapy (ranked #1 by US News and World Report). With annual research expenditures of approximately $20 million, the college currently houses a number of large, interdisciplinary grants with significant funding from the NIH and DoD. The college boasts strong research teams in the areas of neurological disorders, cardiovascular health, chronic disease conditions, molecular biosciences, epidemiology, and public health. The college also houses and supports the Partnership for Health Communities that serves as a community-engagement hub for the University of Delaware. This environment fosters innovative research aimed at addressing pressing health challenges and improving clinical and population health. One of the oldest institutions of higher education in the United States, the University of Delaware has a rich history of contributing scientific insights and new ideas, fostering enlightenment, transforming lives, and changing the world. Today, under the leadership of President Laura Carlson, the University is committed to building on this legacy to meet the complex challenges facing the globe. The University’s foremost priority remains to ensure the success of every student, and this commitment dates to our roots as a small private academy founded in 1743. From that early school, which educated three signers of the Declaration of Independence, UD has grown into a research-intensive, technologically advanced institution that is at the forefront of innovation in the classroom, in the laboratory, and in the community. Located in scenic Newark, Delaware, within 2-3 hours of New York City, Philadelphia, Baltimore, and Washington, D.C., the University is a Land Grant, Sea Grant, Space Grant, and Carnegie Research University (with very high research activity, a designation accorded to fewer than 3% of degree-granting institutions in the U.S.), and with external funding exceeding $200 million. UD has been ranked #43 among the top public schools in the nation by U.S. News & World Report in its 2026 Best Colleges rankings, which evaluate more than 1,700 U.S. colleges and universities. In addition, UD also ranked #88 among all national universities, 55 in the nation for best colleges for veterans, and was recognized as one of the 207 national universities as a Best Value school. A state-assisted, privately governed institution, the University enrolls approximately 19,000 undergraduate and 4,500 graduate students. SALARY AND BENEFITS: Compensation packages are competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Faculty at the University have regularly received annual cost-of-living and merit-based salary increases and the UD benefits package is one of the best in the nation. The University is responsive to the needs of dual-career couples, supports work-life balance through an array of family-friendly policies, and is dedicated to broadening participation in higher education. Relocation funds may be available. The appointment date is set for January or February 2027, with the specific date negotiable. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: Review of applications will occur on a rolling basis and will continue until the position is filled. Nominations and expressions of interest will be held in confidence and references will be contacted only with the candidate’s permission. Applicants should upload a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references at http://www.udel.edu/udjobs/. The letter of application and CV will be shared with the search committee. For inquiries, contact: Jennifer Korkosz, Search Committee Chair, jkorkosz@udel.edu or contact the School of Nursing (302)831-1253. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503944/part-time-graduate-program-support-assistant-college-of-education-human-development": {
      "title": "Part Time Graduate Program Support Assistant, College of Education & Human Development",
      "description": "Part Time Graduate Program Support Assistant, College of Education & Human Development Apply now Job no: 503944 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support Job Title: Part Time Graduate Program Support Assistant Anticipated Start Date: December 1, 2026 Estimated Pay: $25 per hour Schedule: Flexible schedule during business hours Monday to Friday. 8 am to 5 pm Location: Alison Hall West, Room 106 About the Department: The College of Education and Human Development has two academic departments, the School of Education and the Department of Human Development and Family Sciences and supports the Master of Social Work (MSW) program. The Graduate Student Services team in the Dean’s Office provides comprehensive support to graduate students in all stages, from prospective student to the application stage, and then supports matriculated students from new student orientation to graduation. Under the direction of the Senior Assistant Dean for Graduate Student Services and in coordination with other graduate services staff, the department faculty, the Graduate College and UDOnline, the Graduate Program Support Assistant will join the CEHD Graduate Student Services team in providing a full range of student services throughout the graduate student life cycle. CEHD graduate programs include: Master of Social Work (MSW), M.S. and Ph.D. in Human Development and Family Sciences, as well as master's, specialist, and doctoral programs in the School of Education including master’s and specialist programs in Educational Technology, Literacy, School Psychology, Teacher Leadership, Teaching English as a Second Language, and Teaching Students with Disabilities; and doctoral programs including the Ph.D. Education, Ph.D. in Educational Statistics and Research Methods, and the Ed.D. in Organizational Improvement for Educational Leaders. Role Description: This position will help carry out the college's priorities to meet needs of graduate students and provide high-quality services to students in CEHD. Throughout the year, the team has tasks and projects that require a high attention to detail and the ability to see projects through to completion but may change depending on the time of the year. This position is ideal for someone already experienced working in student services in a college or university setting or who is interested in learning a wide range of activities that occur in a dynamic fast-paced academic unit at UD. This position will work in-person on the Newark campus (with some remote work possible), with a flexible schedule possible within typical business hours and join a team of dedicated staff and faculty who support graduate students. Primary Responsibilities: Provide graduate student and program support to a portfolio of programs, with duties including: Respond to inquiries from prospective students, encourage completion of applications, troubleshoot issues, and process admissions decisions as directed by the faculty program director/coordinator. Utilize the Slate application system and CRM for sending messages to prospective and admitted students and provide information to newly admitted students. Provide assistance to non-enrolled students and encourage them to register each semester or request a leave of absence. Answer questions from current students accurately and completely and refer them to appropriate faculty members or campus resources for further assistance. Differentiate communication and support strategies for online and on-campus programs, and those aimed at full-time and part-time, and 4+1 students. Update advisor assignments in UDSIS. Update electronic student files, including at transition points such as admission, graduation or withdrawal. In coordination with program faculty, monitor progress toward degree requirements and maintain student degree audits in Stellic perform such actions as processing course substitutions and exceptions, and entering milestone completion in Stellic. Update expected graduation terms in UDSIS. Process related administrative forms, such as transfer of credit. Review and process Applications for Advanced Degree and confirm completion of degree requirements and eligibility for graduation. Download reports and utilize spreadsheets for student tracking. Prioritize and resolve administrative/procedural problems and issues. Review, analyze, and evaluate work methods and operational procedures for continuous improvement. The portfolio of programs this position primarily supports includes (but is not limited to): M.Ed. Literacy online program , including the new direct admission pathway for qualified alumni M.Ed. in Teacher Leadership online program M.Ed. in Educational Technology program , which includes the online and on-campus programs, and the new and growing 4+1 option for qualified UD undergraduates M.Ed. in Teaching Students with Disabilities program , including the 4+1 option, the MPCP option, the traditional option, and the certificate program. Provide project-based support performing similar functions for other degree programs in CEHD during busy periods, support for tasks such as assisting with assistantship funding letters, course registration assistance, and supporting events. Perform other job-related duties as requested or assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree, and three years of related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, high attention to detail, effective problem-solving skills, and ability to follow projects through to completion. Ability to succeed in a fast-paced environment and enthusiasm for advancing and sharing in the operation of successful programs. Strong ability to interpret, adapt and apply guidelines and procedures. Capacity to interpret data and organize reports. Intermediate level proficiency in computer skills including use of shared Google Docs and Sheets files and drives, Word including mail merges, Excel and presentation software. Occasional evening or weekend work at events is required. Preferred Qualifications UD or higher education experience is preferred, as well as working knowledge of graduate student recruitment techniques, academic policies and procedures, systems, regulations and standards; ability to evaluate student transcripts and/or records and communicate course prerequisites, certification and/or curriculum/graduation requirements are all preferred. Experience with UDSIS, Stellic, and Slate or similar student data, CRM and application systems is preferred. Hiring Timeline: Application Review: November 9-13 Interviews- November 16-20 Offers Extended -November 23-December 4 Please note that dates are subject to change depending on the needs of the department. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Nov 06 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504036/associate-director-crm-administrator-admissions-office": {
      "title": "Associate Director, CRM Administrator, Admissions Office",
      "description": "Associate Director, CRM Administrator, Admissions Office Apply now Job no: 504036 College / VP Area: VP for Enroll Management Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Admissions, Administrative Support Pay Grade: 30E Context of Job: The Office of Admissions at the University of Delaware receives more than 40,000 first year and transfer student applications per year, hosts on-campus and off-campus recruitment events and maintains a prospect list of nearly one million students nationwide and throughout the world. Under the supervision of the Director of Operations, the Associate Director provides strategic leadership for CRM communications, marketing automation, and admissions technology that supports recruitment and enrollment objectives. The Associate Director oversees Slate communication strategy, portal development, analytics, and communication deployment while supervising the Operations Coordinator responsible for admissions mailings. This position serves as the primary functional expert for Slate communications and collaborates with campus partners to develop and implement technology solutions that improve operational efficiency and the prospective and admitted student experience. Major Responsibilities: CRM Management Provides strategic leadership for the admissions CRM, directing communications strategy, infrastructure development, marketing automation, portal enhancements, and implementation and optimization of new system capabilities to improve recruitment and enrollment operations. Develops CRM standards, policies, and best practices while providing leadership, training, and documentation to staff and campus partners to maximize effective system utilization and support Slate best practices. Leads communications deployment strategy, develops scalable communication workflows, and oversees CRM campaigns and A/B testing to improve communication effectiveness while reducing operational complexity. Provides direct supervision, coaching, workload management, and performance evaluation for assigned staff responsible for admissions mailings and communications operations. Provides strategic oversight for admissions portals, including My Blue Hen Home, ensuring an engaging and effective student experience throughout the enrollment lifecycle. Admissions Operations Directs the development and maintenance of communications procedures, process documentation, and operational standards. Reviews and ensures process maps are current and adhere to Enrollment Management policies. Develops and manages special program application processes for new initiatives. Provides leadership for operational planning, workflow management, and continuous process improvement related to admissions communications and mailings. Performs business requirements gathering and analysis and clearly communicates findings and process improvement recommendations to various stakeholders. Systems Directs the resolution of complex CRM data quality issues and recommends long-term system improvements. Develops data collection, analysis, and reporting plans to cover recruitment and admissions activities, enrollment forecasts, enrollment analysis, and the effectiveness of various marketing efforts. Leads the evaluation, implementation, and optimization of CRM technologies that enhance enrollment communications and operational efficiency. Analytics and Reporting Provides strategic oversight for communications analytics and reporting used to inform enrollment planning, marketing decisions, and recruitment, application, and enrollment strategies. Develops enterprise reporting, dashboards, and automated analytics and prepares regular reports on key metrics to support leadership decision making and evaluate communication effectiveness. Creates and generates ongoing recruitment, application, and enrollment reports to support admissions activities, decision making, and enrollment strategies, and supports in-depth analysis of those metrics. Provides strategic reporting and analysis, including comprehensive assessments that interpret patterns and trends and determine overall data quality. Collaborates with Enrollment Management leadership and campus partners to develop CRM communication strategies and data solutions supporting institutional priorities. Performs other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelors degree and four years of related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Advanced knowledge of Slate CRM communications, portals, and marketing automation. Experience leading enterprise CRM initiatives and business process improvement. Demonstrated leadership in project management and cross-functional collaboration. Slate CRM experience required; Tableau and Analytics desired; well-versed in configurable joins and all slate modules with a focus on communications. Well-versed in Google analytics and Ping data analysis to inform decision-making. Experience with assessment and implementation of systematic approaches for communications. Effective writing, oral communication and organizational skills. Proficient computer skills and use of presentation software. Effective interpersonal skills and ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Supervisory experience. Strong presentation skills. Knowledge of student recruitment, retention issues and admissions standards. Ability to multitask, work independently, and work as a team member to achieve common goals. Special Requirements: Ability to work some weekends and evenings. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504039/medical-director-physician-assistant-program": {
      "title": "Medical Director, Physician Assistant Program",
      "description": "Medical Director, Physician Assistant Program Apply now Job no: 504039 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Medical Molecular Sciences University of Delaware Medical Director, Physician Assistant Program Department of Medical & Molecular Sciences The University of Delaware College of Health Sciences (CHS) invites applications for a 12-month, part-time (0.3 FTE, approximately 12 hours per week) position as Medical Director of the new Master of Physician Assistant Studies (MPAS) Program in the Department of Medical & Molecular Sciences (MMSC). This is a unique opportunity for an experienced clinician to join the founding program team and help build a new PA program from the ground up within a top-ranked, research-intensive institution known for its commitment to academic excellence and student success. Reporting to the Program Director, the Medical Director serves as a clinical and professional resource to the program’s faculty, staff, and students, and helps ensure that the curriculum, competencies, and educational experiences reflect current standards of clinical practice and the contemporary role of the physician assistant. This role offers the chance to shape the next generation of PAs, mentor future clinicians, collaborate across the College of Health Sciences, and help prepare PAs to meet healthcare access needs in the Newark and Southern Delaware communities, all while remaining connected to clinical practice. Rank will be determined based on the candidate’s experience. QUALIFICATIONS: Education & Licensure Degree: Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO), or a graduate degree from an accredited physician assistant program. Licensure: Currently or previously licensed as a physician assistant, allopathic physician, or osteopathic physician. Current or eligibility for unencumbered licensure in the State of Delaware preferred. Certification: Currently or previously board certified in their profession (NCCPA for physician assistants) or medical specialty, as applicable. Current certification preferred. Experience Clinical Experience: Clinical experience and professional expertise appropriate to the educational needs of the MPAS Program. Demonstrated knowledge of contemporary clinical practice and the role of physician assistants within healthcare delivery. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, administrators, clinicians, students, and external clinical partners. Experience practicing collaboratively with physician assistants and/or interprofessional healthcare teams preferred. Experience in PA, medical, graduate health professions, or clinical education, including curriculum development, competency-based education, and student assessment, preferred. Familiarity with ARC-PA accreditation standards and processes preferred. Experience serving diverse patient populations, including rural and underserved communities, preferred. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Provide clinical guidance for the development and routine review of the curriculum and program competencies, ensuring didactic and clinical education reflect current practice standards and the role of the physician assistant. Serve as a clinical and professional resource and advisor to the Program Director, faculty, staff, and students. Support clinical education, including the development and evaluation of supervised clinical practice experiences and related policies. Participate in program assessment, continuous quality improvement, and ARC-PA accreditation activities, including site visits. Represent and promote the program, building strong relationships within the University, the healthcare community, and with clinical partners. GENERAL INFORMATION: MMSC is an agile, student-centered department recognized for innovation in education, research, outreach, and practice. Celebrating 75 years, MMSC offers programs in medical laboratory science, medical diagnostics, and biotechnology with a dynamic, federally funded research enterprise ($3.1M+ portfolio). The department enrolls >700 students across four undergraduate majors, including Medical Laboratory Science (NAACLS-accredited) , Applied Molecular Biology & Biotechnology (ASBMB-accredited) , and two Medical Diagnostics majors (one uniquely preparing students for Physician Assistant programs ). Graduate education has grown with three professionally focused master’s degrees alongside our established Ph.D. in Molecular Biosciences. We actively collaborate with academic, clinical, community, and industry partners (60+) to enrich experiential learning and broaden access to STEM. Approximately 50% of MMSC faculty lead sponsored research reflecting our commitment to advancing science and educational innovation. Research space includes the Willard hall, Delaware Biotechnology Institute and the Ammon-Pinizzotto Biopharmaceutical Innovation Center on the University's STAR Campus. The College of Health Sciences is a dynamic, innovative academic unit with 3,400 undergraduate and graduate students and 135 faculty, who are nationally recognized leaders across multiple disciplines. The College is housed in multiple buildings on the traditional main campus but also is an anchor tenant of the University’s Science, Technology, and Advanced Research ( STAR ) Campus. STAR has become an exciting center that combines business, research, education and simulation space, all focused on leading strategic priorities in health science, biopharmaceuticals, data science, engineering, finance and other cross-cutting fields of study. In addition to the Department of MMSC, CHS has six additional academic units, including Communication Sciences and Disorders (#33 U.S. News Best Graduate Schools), Epidemiology, Health Behavior and Nutrition Sciences, Kinesiology and Applied Physiology, Physical Therapy (ranked #1 by US News and World Report), and the School of Nursing with a BSN program ranked #40 and MSN ranked #35 by U.S. News Best Colleges. With annual research expenditures of approximately $20 million, the college currently houses a number of large, interdisciplinary grants with significant funding from the NIH and DoD. The college boasts strong research teams in the areas of neurological disorders, cardiovascular health, chronic disease conditions, molecular biosciences, epidemiology, and public health. The college also houses and supports the Partnership for Health Communities that serves as a community-engagement hub for the University of Delaware. This environment fosters innovative research aimed at addressing pressing health challenges and improving clinical and population health. One of the oldest institutions of higher education in the United States, the University of Delaware has a rich history of contributing scientific insights and new ideas, fostering enlightenment, transforming lives and changing the world. Today under the leadership of President Laura Carlson, the University is committed to building on this legacy to meet the complex challenges facing the globe. The University’s foremost priority remains to ensure the success of every student, and this commitment dates back to our roots as a small private academy founded in 1743. From that early school, which educated three signers of the Declaration of Independence, UD has grown into a research-intensive, technologically advanced institution that is at the forefront of innovation in the classroom, in the laboratory and in the community. Located in scenic Newark, Delaware, within 2 hours of New York, Philadelphia, Baltimore, and Washington, D.C., the University is a Land Grant, Sea Grant, Space Grant, and Carnegie Research University (with very high research activity, a designation accorded to fewer than 3% of degree-granting institutions in the U.S.), and with external funding exceeding $200 million. A state-assisted, privately governed institution, the University enrolls approximately 18,600 undergraduates and 4,500 graduate students. SALARY AND BENEFITS: Compensation packages are competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. The position start date is as soon as possible, but negotiable. Nominations and expressions of interest will be held in confidence, and references will be contacted only with the candidate’s permission. Applicants should upload a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references. The letter of application and CV will be shared with the search committee. For inquiries, please contact Search Co-Chairs: Nicole Pekarek, PhD, MAT, MLS(ASCP)SBB CM , pekarekn@udel.edu and Andrew Hollinger, MS, MLS(ASCP) CM SM CM , ahollin@udel.edu Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503957/part-time-care-coordinator-center-for-counseling-student-development": {
      "title": "Part Time Care Coordinator, Center for Counseling & Student Development",
      "description": "Part Time Care Coordinator, Center for Counseling & Student Development Apply now Job no: 503957 College / VP Area: Vice President for Student Life Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Health Services, Student Affairs & Services Job Title: Part Time Care Coordinator, Center for Counseling & Student Development Anticipated Start Date: October 30, 2026 Estimated Pay: $50.00/hour Schedule: Day: 24 hours/week Location: Newark, DE; Warner Hall Role Description: Under the direction of the Assistant Director for Care Coordination, the Care Coordinator provides care navigation services to students needing resources outside of CCSD for routine, urgent, and emergent concerns. The Care Coordinator is tasked with supporting the Assistant Director for Care Coordination in ensuring a network of support is identified and in place for students, with a special emphasis on students in crisis. The Care Coordinator will support the Assistant Director for Care Coordination to follow students who have been referred for higher level of care evaluations and ensures adequate return to campus plans. The Care Coordinator will participate in student-facing mental health, suicide, and substance use disorder outreach. Efforts are inclusive of reducing stigma, raising awareness, providing education, screening, and referral resources. The Care Coordinator will cultivate stakeholder relationships as well. Please note: This position is funded [in part] under grant number SM-26-009 (GLS Campus Suicide Prevention Grant Program) from the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA), U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS). The views, policies, and opinions expressed are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect those of SAMHSA or HHS. Continuation of this role is contingent upon the renewal of external grant funding. Primary Responsibilities: Establishes relationships with on-campus and community partners to gain awareness of the intake, discharge process for hospitals and intensive outpatient programs (IOPs), and knowledge of services provided by various on-campus partners including Student Health Services, Student Wellness and Health Promotion, and other offices when appropriate. Provides appropriate referrals and case management based on communication and consultation with professionals to ensure the safety of students/clients. Acts as advocate/liaison between client, client's CCSD therapist, client’s referral therapist/s or clinic/hospital, and client’s health insurance provider. Consults with students, professionals, and faculty related to care navigation. Maintains case records in a confidential manner adhering to FERPA regulations. Maintains up-to-date knowledge of State and Federal acts such as State of Delaware Child Abuse Reporting laws, FERPA, Title IX, and Clery Act. Provides outreach to others on campus as a representative of the center. Maintains and executes the on-going review of referral outcomes for our students from both the students’ and the providers’ perspective. Collects and analyzes data related to case management and referral. Develops and engages with local and regional mental health providers regarding factors such as areas of specialty, insurance policies, and availability of service for a wide variety of community resources that support the student/client’s efforts to grow and change. Maintains a web-based database of local providers for referral purposes. Coordinate and lead student-facing outreach, screening, and prevention efforts as directed. Responsibility for addressing problems and seeing solutions through to completion in compliance with rules, regulations, and laws. Provides outreach to others on campus as a representative of the center. Serves on committees and/or performs special projects as needed. Performs miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. May require periodic evening or weekend hours Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required, masters preferred in the following nursing, social work, counseling (licensed or licensed eligible in the state of Delaware). Crisis management and case management experience Excellent interpersonal skills including the ability to communicate effectively and develop and maintain stakeholder relationships. Knowledge of mental health and substance use disorders, suicide prevention, young adult development, and learning theories. Ability to manage multiple priorities and complete tasks in a timely manner. Ability to analyze problems and develop creative solutions to complex issues. Knowledge of and ability to grow awareness of area social services, treatment facilities, supportive services, and practitioners. Experience with clinical documentation and using electronic health records. Proficiency in computer skills and web navigation abilities. Demonstrated commitment to team collaboration. Hiring Timeline: Start Date: ASAP/10.30.2026 Please note that dates are subject to change depending on the needs of the department. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Nov 20 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503996/founding-program-director-physician-assistant-program-medical-molecular-sciences-mmsc": {
      "title": "Founding Program Director, Physician Assistant Program, Medical & Molecular Sciences (MMSC)",
      "description": "Founding Program Director, Physician Assistant Program, Medical & Molecular Sciences (MMSC) Apply now Job no: 503996 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Faculty Location: Newark, DE Categories: Medical Molecular Sciences University of Delaware Physician Assistant Program Director Department of Medical & Molecular Sciences The University of Delaware College of Health Sciences (CHS) invites applications for a full-time, open-rank, continuing track faculty position as the Founding Program Director, Physician Assistant Program in the Department of Medical & Molecular Sciences (MMSC). This is a unique opportunity for an experienced, accomplished, and innovative leader to shape and grow a new PA program within a top-ranked, research-intensive institution known for its commitment to academic excellence and student success. The Founding Program Director will have the chance to build a diverse and high-quality faculty team, design an innovative curriculum, develop policy aligned with ARC-PA standards, and lead the program through initial accreditation and ongoing excellence. This role offers the ability to influence the future of PA education while collaborating across the College of Health Sciences, engaging with the Newark and Southern Delaware communities, and contributing to a nationally recognized institution with a commitment to professional preparation, experiential learning, and student success. Candidates must hold a master’s degree in related health field, education or science; a doctoral degree is preferred. Candidate must also hold current certification from the National Commission on Certification of Physician Assistants (NCCPA) and possess, or be eligible for, unencumbered licensure as a physician assistant in Delaware. The successful candidate will bring a minimum of three years of full-time clinical experience and three years of full-time higher education experience in an instructional or leadership capacity within a PA educational program. Candidates must demonstrate a strong working knowledge of ARC-PA accreditation requirements and standards, including experience with self-study preparation, accreditation site visits, and ongoing compliance. The successful candidate will lead the development and advancement of the physician assistant program, guiding preparation for provisional accreditation and subsequent accreditation reviews, ensuring compliance with ARC-PA standards, and directing continuous program assessment and improvement. In collaboration with faculty and University partners, the Program Director will oversee student recruitment and enrollment, curriculum delivery, course scheduling, faculty assignments, and student support. The Program Director will recruit, supervise, and mentor faculty and staff, cultivating a collegial academic environment that supports collaboration and professional development. The Program Director will also oversee program administration, budget, and resources; develop and maintain clinical affiliate partnerships that support student placements; and represent the program within the University and the broader professional community. Through these partnerships, the director will advance the program’s reputation and strategic growth. GENERAL INFORMATION: MMSC is an agile, student-centered department recognized for innovation in education, research, outreach, and practice. Celebrating 75 years, MMSC offers programs in medical laboratory science, medical diagnostics, and biotechnology with a dynamic, federally funded research enterprise ($3.1M+ portfolio). The department enrolls >700 students across four undergraduate majors, including Medical Laboratory Science (NAACLS-accredited), Applied Molecular Biology & Biotechnology (ASBMB-accredited), and two Medical Diagnostics majors (one uniquely preparing students for Physician Assistant programs). Graduate education has grown with three professionally focused master’s degrees alongside our established Ph.D. in Molecular Biosciences. We actively collaborate with academic, clinical, community, and industry partners (60+) to enrich experiential learning and broaden access to STEM. Approximately 50% of MMSC faculty lead sponsored research reflecting our commitment to advancing science and educational innovation. Research space includes the Willard Hall, Delaware Biotechnology Institute and the Ammon-Pinizzotto Biopharmaceutical Innovation Center on the University's STAR Campus. The College of Health Sciences is a dynamic, innovative academic unit with 3,400 undergraduate and graduate students and 135 faculty, who are nationally recognized leaders across multiple disciplines. The College is housed in multiple buildings on the traditional main campus but also is an anchor tenant of the University’s Science, Technology, and Advanced Research ( STAR ) Campus. STAR has become an exciting center that combines business, research, education and simulation space, all focused on leading strategic priorities in health science, biopharmaceuticals, data science, engineering, finance and other cross-cutting fields of study. In addition to the Department of MMSC, CHS has six additional academic units, including Communication Sciences and Disorders (#33 U.S. News Best Graduate Schools), Epidemiology, Health Behavior and Nutrition Sciences, Kinesiology and Applied Physiology, Physical Therapy (ranked #1 by US News and World Report), and the School of Nursing with a BSN program ranked #40 and MSN ranked #35 by U.S. News Best Colleges. With annual research expenditures of approximately $20 million, the college currently houses a number of large, interdisciplinary grants with significant funding from the NIH and DoD. The college boasts strong research teams in the areas of neurological disorders, cardiovascular health, chronic disease conditions, molecular biosciences, epidemiology, and public health. The college also houses and supports the Partnership for Health Communities that serves as a community-engagement hub for the University of Delaware. This environment fosters innovative research aimed at addressing pressing health challenges and improving clinical and population health. One of the oldest institutions of higher education in the United States, the University of Delaware has a rich history of contributing scientific insights and new ideas, fostering enlightenment, transforming lives and changing the world. Today under the leadership of President Laura Carlson, the University is committed to building on this legacy to meet the complex challenges facing the globe. The University’s foremost priority remains to ensure the success of every student, and this commitment dates back to our roots as a small private academy founded in 1743. From that early school, which educated three signers of the Declaration of Independence, UD has grown into a research-intensive, technologically advanced institution that is at the forefront of innovation in the classroom, in the laboratory and in the community. Located in scenic Newark, Delaware, within 2 hours of New York, Philadelphia, Baltimore, and Washington, D.C., the University is a Land Grant, Sea Grant, Space Grant, and Carnegie Research University (with very high research activity, a designation accorded to fewer than 3% of degree-granting institutions in the U.S.), and with external funding exceeding $200 million. A state-assisted, privately governed institution, the University enrolls approximately 18,600 undergraduates and 4,500 graduate students. SALARY AND BENEFITS: Compensation packages are competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Faculty at the University have regularly received annual cost-of-living and merit salary increases and the UD benefits package is one of the best in the nation. The University is responsive to the needs of dual career couples, supports work-life balance through an array of family-friendly policies, and is dedicated to broadening participation in higher education. Relocation funds and start-up funds are available. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. The position start date is as soon as possible, but negotiable. Nominations and expressions of interest will be held in confidence and references will be contacted only with the candidate’s permission. Applicants should upload a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references at http://www.udel.edu/udjobs/. The letter of application and CV will be shared with the search committee. For inquiries, please contact Search Co-Chairs: Nicole Pekarek, PhD, MAT, MLS(ASCP)SBB CM , pekarekn@udel.edu and Andrew Hollinger, MS, MLS(ASCP) CM SM CM , ahollin@udel.edu Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503992/academic-program-coordinator-history": {
      "title": "Academic Program Coordinator, History",
      "description": "Academic Program Coordinator, History Apply now Job no: 503992 College / VP Area: College of Arts & Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support PAY GRADE: 29E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The College of Arts and Sciences (CAS) is the largest college at the University of Delaware, comprising 22 academic departments, 2 academic centers, and 37 interdisciplinary centers, programs, and institutes across the arts, humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences., CAS plays a central role in the University’s academic mission, providing foundational coursework for all UD students and serving more than 8,400 undergraduate and graduate majors. CAS is home to over 520 full-time faculty who are nationally and internationally recognized scholars and innovative teachers. With annual sponsored research expenditures exceeding $40 million, the College supports significant interdisciplinary research and maintains outstanding teaching and research facilities. Supported by over 425 staff members and 45 postdoctoral fellows and researchers, CAS operates with annual expenditures of over $152 million and serves as a hub for interdisciplinary engagement, connecting the University with local, national, and global communities through research, creative activity, and public service. The Department of History is housed within the College of Arts and Sciences and offers nationally recognized graduate and undergraduate programs. The Department currently consists of 24 tenure track, continuing track, and temporary faculty, two administrative staff, approximately 225 undergraduate majors, and 42 graduate students. Annual departmental research expenditures total approximately $0.125M per year, with a total department budget of over $6M per year. Under the limited direction of the department Business Administrator (BA) and in coordination with the department Chairperson(s), the Academic Program Coordinator (APC) is responsible for the coordination of departmental academic programs and a wide range of department, faculty, and staff operational needs and activities. The Coordinator interacts with and supports faculty, students, College and University administrators, and alumni to support the mission and goals of the Department(s), College, and University and to advance departmental teaching, service, and research objectives. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Manage operational aspects of the departmental undergraduate and graduate programs to ensure student progress toward degree completion through both internal departmental documentation and the Stellic degree management and advising platform. Assist graduate and undergraduate students and faculty advisors with registration and scheduling issues, drop/add, overrides, transfer credit evaluations, change of registration without penalty, registration holds, and general advisement questions, in coordination with department faculty, the BA, the College Student Success Center (SSC) team, the Graduate College, and the Honors College. Meet with walk-in visitors, including prospective students (high school, new admits) and their parents/guardians regarding academic requirements, career opportunities and University resources. Respond to inquiries, investigate, and resolve problems related to undergraduate and graduate transactions handled by the department. Interpret and explain College and University policies, practices, and procedures to parents/guardians and prospective students as well as to current students and faculty. Develop curriculum guides to address issues unique to department’s majors and minors. Manage departmental course scheduling, in coordination with department faculty and the BA. Input all course data into UDSIS and manage classroom requests to secure rooms and avoid conflicts, in conjunction with other departments. Resolve conflicts of departmental courses with each other and with courses outside the department that are part of the departmental curriculum, and monitor enrollments to determine whether more sections need to be added or cancelled. Validate grade roster and faculty workload, approve change of Academic Program Plan webforms, make faculty advisor assignments, and coordinate final exam schedules and course evaluations. Monitor progress towards degree for departmental graduate students. Work with faculty advisors to track and prepare for qualifying exams, dissertation proposals, and dissertation defenses. Coordinate with the Graduate College and resolves issues as they arise. Liaison to department faculty to edit the UD Course Catalog to reflect all changes in undergraduate and graduate course descriptions and degree programs and submits descriptions of proposed new courses and curriculum changes for approval. Participate in undergraduate and graduate recruitment efforts by advising applicants on general academic requirements, managing annual graduate recruitment events, and coordinating the Department's participation in a variety of special undergraduate admissions events including Admitted Students Days and Blue & Golden Saturdays for prospective students, parents/guardians, faculty and others. Coordinate with SSC and department Chair on graduate and undergraduate New Student Orientation. Manage and update students' files and records. Check various forms for accuracy such as grade checks, overload petitions, and curriculum changes; assists students in the completion of required forms that may contain confidential information. Maintain the department's undergraduate and graduate files including but not limited to course syllabi, course descriptions, curriculum changes, enrollment records, and schedule records. Supports faculty requests for UDSIS information regarding student academic issues and for referencing unofficial transcripts. Conduct and/or facilitate data analysis as needed for the purpose of curriculum planning or assessment. Manage daily operations of the Department including: Manage event planning for department functions and special events, such as speaker series, faculty recruitment, and convocation, in coordination with College Events staff. Direct, maintain, and process department payroll for student workers, miscellaneous wage employees, and hourly graduate students. Review timesheets for accuracy, appropriate financial information, and runs ad hoc reports as needed. Resolve concerns with College HR as needed. Provide administrative support for internal communications, including updating internal intranet sites, internal newsletters, internal mailing lists, event promotion to internal audiences, and routine correspondence with faculty, staff, and students. Liaise with the CAS Communications Team to access communications services. Provide administrative support for Department Chair and faculty, as needed. Attend and take notes at departmental committee meetings for the undergraduate and graduate programs. Assume responsibility for other operational needs within the department, such as supply maintenance and standard equipment maintenance, donor acknowledgement letters, exam copying coordination, departmental mail, and records retention and filing needs. Assist with special projects and performs other job-related duties as assigned. With the assistance of the CAS Financial Operations team, oversee the daily processing and accounting for revenue and expenditures. Ensure accuracy and availability of funding and works with the Financial Operations team to maintain compliance with accounting procedures. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration or a related discipline and three years of job-related work experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience Working knowledge of undergraduate and graduate academic and recruitment policies, procedures, systems, regulations, and standards at the University level preferred. Ability to evaluate student transcripts and/or records. Knowledge of graduate academic standards and student conduct polices governing academic misconduct and student recruitment and retention issues Ability to communicate course prerequisites, certification, and/or curriculum/graduation requirements to students Effective organizational skills Ability to interpret, adapt, and apply guidelines and procedures Ability to communicate effectively and interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds Ability to thrive in a fast-paced environment Commitment to advancing and sharing successful programs and initiatives Ability to grasp, retain, and disseminate an extensive and varied body of information; the ability to exercise sound judgment and employ effective decision-making skills Ability to gather and analyze statistical and financial data and generate ad-hoc reports. A minimum of intermediate level proficiency with spreadsheets and databases Experience with University's student records system (UDSIS), degree management and advising system (Stellic), graduate application database (SLATE), and accounting system (based on PeopleSoft) preferred Ability to manage multiple priorities, complex projects, and long-term initiatives with precision Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504011/research-associate-i-biological-sciences": {
      "title": "Research Associate I, Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Research Associate I, Biological Sciences Apply now Job no: 504011 College / VP Area: College of Arts & Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory Pay Grade: 28N Department: Biological Sciences College/Admin Office: Arts & Sciences Location: Newark, DE Context of Job: The College of Arts and Sciences (CAS) is the largest college at the University of Delaware, comprising 22 academic departments, 2 academic centers, and 37 interdisciplinary centers, programs, and institutes across the arts, humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences. CAS plays a central role in the University’s academic mission, providing foundational coursework for all UD students and serving more than 8,400 undergraduate and graduate majors. CAS is home to over 520 full-time faculty who are nationally and internationally recognized scholars and innovative teachers. With annual sponsored research expenditures exceeding $40 million, the College supports significant interdisciplinary research and maintains outstanding teaching and research facilities. Supported by over 425 staff members and 45 postdoctoral fellows and researchers, CAS operates with annual expenditures of over $152 million and serves as a hub for interdisciplinary engagement, connecting the University with local, national, and global communities through research, creative activity, and public service. The Department of Biological Sciences is housed within the College of Arts and Sciences and offers nationally recognized undergraduate and graduate programs. The department currently consists of 37 faculty members, approximately 714 undergraduate majors, and 104 graduate students. Annual department research expenditures total approximately $4.3M per year, with a total fiscal responsibility of $16.8M per year. The unit also supports the Interdisciplinary Science Learning Labs (ISLL). Under the general direction and supervision of the Principal Investigators (PI), the Research Associate I will participate in and support daily research activities in the Song Lab. The principal emphasis is managing inventories, maintaining marine animal systems, and supporting cutting-edge research. Working directly with the Principal Investigator (PI), postdocs, and students, the Research Associate I will assist with ongoing collaborative projects. Major Responsibilities: Assist Principal Investigators in the analyses and interpretation of the experiment results. Collect, and compile data; assist with statistical and narrative reports and/or graphs. Maintain supply inventories, organize reagents and experimental protocols, and manage laboratory stock. Maintain aquaria and care for live marine organisms used in active research projects; maintain cultured cell lines as needed. Work closely with the PI, postdocs, graduate students, and undergraduates on team experiments. Assist with research projects, including reading and synthesizing scientific literature to help inform experimental design and troubleshooting. Calibrate, set up, and maintain lab equipment; perform digital image processing; and ensure accurate data collection and record-keeping. Comply with Department of Environmental Health and Safety, OSHA, and biohazard safety standards and promote workplace safety. Maintain sophisticated laboratory tests/apparatus; perform and oversee experiments and data collection to ensure data integrity and protocol compliance. Assist graduate and undergraduate students regarding good safety practices with particular attention to monitoring students in the use of hazardous materials. Utilize information taken from primary journal articles to carry out relevant experiments and or interpretation of results. Present experimental results to the faculty supervisor or to the lab group for discussion and future planning. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Biology, Developmental Biology, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, RNA Biology, or a closely related discipline or a related field with one year of lab experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Highly organized and attention to details. Hands-on bench experience, marine organism care, or equivalent upper-level academic laboratory coursework. Basic proficiency in molecular biology techniques (e.g., PCR, gel electrophoresis, nucleic acid extractions). Familiarity with standard office software (MS Word, Excel, PowerPoint). Experience with—or willingness to learn—image processing using Zeiss Zen software and Adobe Creative Suite (Illustrator, Photoshop). Knowledge of the use and maintenance of laboratory facilities and/or equipment. Knowledge of scientific approach and methodologies. Ability to understand, follow, and enforce safety procedures. Ability to organize and coordinate scientific and/or engineering research projects; as well as ability to develop and follow research methodology and protocol. Ability to work effectively with faculty, staff and students. Environmental/Occupational Exposures: Required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to hazardous materials. Special Requirements: With or without assistance, must be able to lift up to 20 lbs. Requires dexterity in handling delicate and hazardous materials as well as sensitive laboratory equipment. The ability to accurately discern colors and measure laboratory reagents. Must be able to work various and sometimes long hours due to the timing on experiments, including possibly holidays, weekends and evenings. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504013/financial-processor-ii-cas-financial-operations": {
      "title": "Financial Processor II, CAS Financial Operations",
      "description": "Financial Processor II, CAS Financial Operations Apply now Job no: 504013 College / VP Area: College of Arts & Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Financial Services and Procurement PAY GRADE: 28N CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The College of Arts and Sciences (CAS) is the largest college at the University of Delaware, comprising 22 academic departments, 2 academic centers, and 37 interdisciplinary centers, programs, and institutes across the arts, humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences., CAS plays a central role in the University’s academic mission, providing foundational coursework for all UD students and serving more than 8,400 undergraduate and graduate majors. CAS is home to over 520 full-time faculty who are nationally and internationally recognized scholars and innovative teachers. With annual sponsored research expenditures exceeding $40 million, the College supports significant interdisciplinary research and maintains outstanding teaching and research facilities. Supported by over 425 staff members and 45 postdoctoral fellows and researchers, CAS operates with annual expenditures of over $152 million and serves as a hub for interdisciplinary engagement, connecting the University with local, national, and global communities through research, creative activity, and public service. Under the limited supervision of the Manager, Financial Processing and in coordination with the Unit Business Administrators, the Financial Processor II performs a complex range of financial activities. The principal emphasis is on the management of financial record activities associated with the procurement and financial operations of the College of Arts and Sciences. A high level of independent judgment is required to ensure financial record system accuracy, appropriate application of inventory control procedures, and University policy compliance. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Purchasing Management: Coordinates and books domestic & international travel for University faculty, students, and guests; ensuring university travel practices and guidelines are followed. Maintains detailed records of departmental funding and travel itineraries. Collaborate with departmental staff for specific needs and expectations. Administers and processes all purchasing requisitions to include purchase orders and non-PO payments such as honorarium, awards, payments to individuals and vendors, participant support/incentive, legal payment, patent, leased space, etc. Ensures that all contract and grant expenses are allowable, allocable, reasonable, and allocated as budgeted, in compliance with policies and procedures, and in collaboration with commodity approvers and the College’s Sponsored Programs Team. Processes requisitions for capital equipment in collaboration with commodity approver and CAS Research Administration. Identify and track fabrication transactions for capitalization, process journal vouchers for fabrication and coordinate asset inventory tagging with Department Business Administrators and CAS Research Administration. Maintains detailed and itemized receipts and supporting documentation for all transactions and expenses. Serves as the independent reviewer of all credit card transactions. Serve as a departmental/college liaison with UDs Procurement Department. Trains department/college credit card holders for compliance travel, research, and other applicable financial policies, procedures, and systems. Ensures all cardholders understand policies and procedures concerning credit card usage. Processes applications for department-issued/college-issued credit cards, cash advance cards, and other travel forms. Travel & Business Hosting Management: Administers and processes all Travel and Business hosting requisitions to include travel request and reimbursement to individuals. Ensures that all contract and grant expenses are allowable, allocable, reasonable, and allocated as budgeted, in compliance with policies and procedures. Maintains detailed and itemized receipts and supporting documentation for all transactions and expenses. Serves as the independent reviewer of all credit card transactions. Serves as a departmental/college liaison with UDs Procurement Department. Financial Management: Identifies financial errors including allocations and transactions and process the necessary forms to correct allocation of expenses to the appropriate fund. Allocates and transfers revenue and expenses between Department accounts by processing Journal Vouchers. Ensures timely processing of all transactions through weekly reporting and coordinates processing with approvers, commodity approvers and UD Procurement. Resolves any inquires made by faculty, staff, and students regarding travel, research, and other financial expenditures and payments. Escalates issues to supervisor or Department Business Administrator as needed. Creates ad-hoc reports and performs other job-related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS: High School diploma or GED, Associates degree preferred, and four years of related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Ability to analyze and interpret financial data, make independent decisions and judgments in keeping with the level of the position. Ability to handle multiple assignments concurrently. Must have strong organizational skills with attention to detail. Ability to use Excel and procurement software. Knowledge of the principles and practices of office management. Knowledge of financial, accounting, grant, and administrative systems, policies and procedures preferred. Working knowledge of University financial and operating policies and procedures preferred. Familiarity with PeopleSoft is preferred. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504019/part-time-assistant-livein-coordinator-edge-summer-college-program": {
      "title": "Part Time Assistant Live-In Coordinator, Edge Summer College Program",
      "description": "Part Time Assistant Live-In Coordinator, Edge Summer College Program Apply now Job no: 504019 College / VP Area: VP for Enroll Management Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Admissions Job Title: Part Time Assistant Live-In Coordinator, Edge Summer College Program Anticipated Start Date: July 13, 2027 Anticipated End Date: August 7, 2027 Compensation: Staff will be paid $40 per hour over the course of 3.5 weeks. Estimated hours 35-40/week. Schedule: Schedule will vary and be determined during training week in mid-July 2027. Location: Center Campus Department: Office of Undergraduate Admissions Role Description: The Edge Summer College Program, led by UD’s Office of Undergraduate Admissions, is seeking dedicated, motivated, responsible, and student-centered individuals to serve as Assistant Live-In Coordinators for the summer 2027 program. The Assistant Live-In Coordinators, under the supervision of the Director and Assistant Director of Pre-College Programs, will lead the residential community and provide a safe, healthy, and inclusive community for three and a half weeks. Please note that this position is temporary and seasonal. Primary Responsibilities: To manage day to day operations of the residential area during designated duty shifts. To assist the Program Director and Assistant Director with residence hall preparation, check-in/check-out process, as well as addressing student concerns and issues, conflict mediation, compliance with university policies, and more. To serve as a liaison to all peer staff but specifically to the Residential component, and ensure duties of undergraduate mentors are carried out (including but not limited to evening activities, RA duty shifts, etc.) To be available to work throughout the duration of the program, including chaperoning all weekend trips and programming. Carry out program policies and rules to ensure student safety, health, wellness, and compliance with UD’s protection of minors policies. Uphold high standards of personal and professional conduct by adhering to UD’s protection of minors policies and appropriate behavior while engaging with minors and Edge peer staff. Live-in staff are also expected to abstain from the use, possession, or influence of alcohol, illegal drugs, or other controlled substances while performing duties and residing on UD’s campus. Please note that failure to adhere to these expectations may result in disciplinary action, including immediate relief of duties from the position. Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree or higher 2-3 years of experience working with minors in settings such as camps, after-school programs, youth organizations, K–12 schools, residential programs, or pre-college summer programs. Previous experience in residential life, live-in student staff leadership, or community management preferred. Hiring Timeline: Application Opens: October 28, 2026 Application Due Date: February 2, 2027 Interview Notification: February 13, 2027 by 5:00 p.m. Interviews: Week of February 16, 2027 Notification of Selection: February 27, 2027 by 5:00 p.m. Deadline to Accept Position (for those offered a position): March 6, 2027 by 5:00 p.m. Please note that dates are subject to change depending on the needs of the department. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Feb 02 2027 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504025/part-time-assistant-livein-coordinator-college-readiness-scholars-institute-crsi-program": {
      "title": "Part Time Assistant Live-In Coordinator, College Readiness Scholars Institute (CRSI) Program",
      "description": "Part Time Assistant Live-In Coordinator, College Readiness Scholars Institute (CRSI) Program Apply now Job no: 504025 College / VP Area: VP for Enroll Management Work type: Staff, Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support Job Title: Part Time Assistant Live-In Coordinator, College Readiness Scholars Institute (CRSI) Program Anticipated Start Date: June 15, 2027 Anticipated End Date: July 3, 2027 Estimated Pay: Staff will be paid $40 per hour over the course of 2.5 weeks. Estimated hours 35-40/week. Schedule: Schedule will vary and be determined during training week in mid-June 2027. Location: Center Campus About the department / center: Office of Undergraduate Admissions Role Description: The College Readiness Scholars Institute (CRSI) program, led by UD’s Office of Undergraduate Admissions, is seeking dedicated, motivated, responsible, and student-centered individuals to serve as Assistant Live-In Coordinators for the summer 2027 program. The Assistant Live-In Coordinators, under the supervision of the Director and Assistant Director of Pre-College Programs, will lead the residential community and provide a safe, healthy, and inclusive community for two and a half weeks. Please note that this position is temporary and seasonal. Primary Responsibilities: To manage day to day operations of the residential area during designated duty shifts. To assist the Program Director and Assistant Director with residence hall preparation, check-in/check-out process, as well as addressing student concerns and issues, conflict mediation, compliance with university policies, and more. To serve as a liaison to all peer staff but specifically to the Residential component, and ensure duties of undergraduate mentors are carried out (including but not limited to evening activities, RA duty shifts, etc.) To be available to work throughout the duration of the program, including chaperoning all weekend trips and programming. Carry out program policies and rules to ensure student safety, health, wellness, and compliance with UD’s protection of minors policies. Provide additional day time support during the Academic and Interpersonal components as needed. Uphold high standards of personal and professional conduct by adhering to UD’s protection of minors policies and appropriate behavior while engaging with minors and CRSI peer staff. Live-in staff are also expected to abstain from the use, possession, or influence of alcohol, illegal drugs, or other controlled substances while performing duties and residing on UD’s campus. Please note that failure to adhere to these expectations may result in disciplinary action, including immediate relief of duties from the position. Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree or higher 2-3 years of experience working with minors in settings such as camps, after-school programs, youth organizations, K–12 schools, residential programs, or pre-college summer programs. Previous experience in residential life, live-in student staff leadership, or community management preferred. Hiring Timeline: Application Opens: October 28, 2026 Application Due Date: February 2, 2027 Interview Notification: February 13, 2027 by 5:00 p.m. Interviews: Week of February 16, 2027 Notification of Selection : February 27, 2027 by 5:00 p.m. Deadline to Accept Position (for those offered a position): March 6, 2027 by 5:00 p.m. Please note that dates are subject to change depending on the needs of the department. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Feb 02 2027 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
