    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504038/master-electronicsinstrument-specialist-chemistry-biology": {
      "title": "Master Electronics/Instrument Specialist, Chemistry & Biology",
      "description": "Master Electronics/Instrument Specialist, Chemistry & Biology Apply now Job no: 504038 College / VP Area: College of Arts & Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory PAY GRADE: 29N CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The College of Arts and Sciences (CAS) is the largest college at the University of Delaware, comprising 22 academic departments, 2 academic centers, and 37 interdisciplinary centers, programs, and institutes across the arts, humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences., CAS plays a central role in the University’s academic mission, providing foundational coursework for all UD students and serving more than 8,400 undergraduate and graduate majors. CAS is home to over 520 full-time faculty who are nationally and internationally recognized scholars and innovative teachers. With annual sponsored research expenditures exceeding $40 million, the College supports significant interdisciplinary research and maintains outstanding teaching and research facilities. Supported by over 425 staff members and 45 postdoctoral fellows and researchers, CAS operates with annual expenditures of over $152 million and serves as a hub for interdisciplinary engagement, connecting the University with local, national, and global communities through research, creative activity, and public service. The Department of Chemistry & Biochemistry is housed within the College of Arts and Sciences and offers nationally recognized undergraduate and graduate programs. The department currently consists of 34 faculty members, approximately 200 undergraduate majors, and 160 graduate students. Annual department research expenditures total approximately $7.5M per year, with a total fiscal responsibility of $23M per year. Under the general supervision of the Manager, Technical Services, the Master Instrument Specialist performs highly skilled and complex technical work associated with installing, calibrating, maintaining, and repairing this instrumentation. The ideal candidate will have a broad understanding of mechanical, electromechanical, fluid and gas control systems, as well as the willingness and ability to continually learn about an ever-expanding range of equipment in a growing and dynamic research environment. Independent judgment is required in the application of established policies and procedures to solve problems associated with this diverse range of instrumentation. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Design, or assist in the design, development and operation of highly specialized laboratory equipment and instrumentation. Perform maintenance, repair, modification, and installation of sophisticated instrumentation and related electromechanical, vacuum, gas, liquid and cryogenic systems and components. Assist with laboratory preparations for instrument installations/relocations, including assembly of manifolds and installation of tubing from compressed gas sources to equipment. Calibrate scientific equipment following detailed procedures to achieve required performance specifications. Locate and rectify problems in high-pressure components of chromatographic equipment; replace pump seals, check valves and fittings as needed. Assist with the design, fabrication, assembly, and installation of structures from steel, aluminum, polymers, composites, plexi-glass, brass, copper, and other materials as appropriate for each project. Provide technical support for teaching laboratories; including repairing, installing, and relocating equipment as needed, and advising faculty, staff, and students on the proper and safe use of laboratory equipment and instrumentation. Perform intricate alignment of optics and assemblies on spectrophotometers, fluorometers, diffractometers, mass spectrometers and other highly sensitive instrumentation. Interpret and prepare schematics and/or drawings and obtain service manuals and technical information from manufacturers. Install and configure software on data collection systems to establish communication and upgrade data processing capabilities of chemical analysis instruments. Install and test electromechanical devices and related controllers using soldering equipment, hand and power tools and various test equipment. Advise students and staff on the installation and use of research equipment by developing instruction aids when needed. Procure, issue and maintain shop test equipment, tools and inventory of parts. Perform other related duties as assigned. OCCUPATIONAL EXPOSURES: Required to use personal protective equipment, such as safety goggles for ultraviolet radiation, to prevent exposure to hazardous materials. QUALIFICATIONS: High school diploma, GED, or vocational technical school certificate and five years of experience building or repairing mechanical, electromechanical and/or electronic equipment, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Skill in maintenance and repair of mechanical, electronic and/or electromechanical equipment. Ability to disassemble and reassemble devices containing fragile and/or miniature components and wiring. Knowledge of fluid and gas fittings, valves, and piping systems. Experience with metal tube bending a plus. Experience in skilled trades, such as carpentry, plumbing, and/or electrical work, is preferred. Ability to use conventional machinery and/or equipment such as drill press, sanders, and grinders as well as hand and sheet metal tools. Experience with 80/20 or other framing systems a plus. Ability to work independently; ability to anticipate and proactively solve problems. Ability to handle multiple priorities at once and work effectively under pressure. Ability to communicate effectively and interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds. SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS: Ability to visually color code wire, size conduit and troubleshoot related problems. Ability to lift and move, without assistance, equipment weighting up to 50 pounds on a regular basis. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504040/grants-analyst-i-college-of-education-and-human-development": {
      "title": "Grants Analyst I, College of Education and Human Development",
      "description": "Grants Analyst I, College of Education and Human Development Apply now Job no: 504040 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Grants & Contracts Pay Grade: 29E Context of Job: Under the general direction of the Senior Grants Analyst with support of Grants Analyst II within the Financial Services Unit (FSU), the Grants Analyst supports pre and post award activities relating to budget planning, development, control, and administration of financial operations within the College of Education and Human Development (CEHD). They actively collaborate and maintain productive relationships with constituencies related to contract and grant operations. The Grants Analyst will provide support to develop competitive grant proposals, assist in preparation of project budgets, and ensure that submission guidelines are followed. They will apply University processes and provide analysis to track and manage financial activities to ensure compliance with University, College, Federal, state and sponsor agency guidelines. Major Responsibilities: Pre-Award Assist with development of competitive proposals to secure independent funding for projects. Manage internal funding applications and select external portfolio types. Pasticipate in meetings to discuss timeline and checklist of sponsor requirements in preparation of submission to ensure completion of proposal materials including budgets, statement of work and other sponsor and Research Office required documents. Ensure all components of proposal including budget, budget justification, and support documents are correctly entered into PeopleSoft and other sponsor sites as required per solicitation. Assist with coordination and submission of complete proposal package. Post-Award Support monthly account summaries of budget, expenses and obligations for select grants and contracts. Analyze and evaluate awarded grants, contracts, and subcontract requirements during the life of award to maintain consistency with terms and conditions to reduce the chance of an audit finding. Review, approve and monitor grant expenditures and documentation to ensure they are processed in a timely manner. Ensure expenses are allowable, allocable, and reasonable based on project, budgetary, sponsor and University guidelines and Uniform Guidance. Support award no cost extensions, budget reallocations and supplemental funding requests. Review and support tracking of personnel effort through the Labor Allocation Module to reflect project commitments and budget constraints. Support required sponsor financial reports (quarterly, annually, etc.) and award close-out. Act as backup for Financial Processor and assist with purchases and reconciliation as required. Act as FSU liaison on various tasks such as Delegated Authorities and Talent Link new hire entries. Support the preparation, processing, approvals and reconciliation of journal vouchers, purchasing cards, purchase orders, web forms (vendor payments, payments to individuals, cash transmittals, payroll). Assist in complying with audit requests from the Research Office, University or outside organizations. Recommend changes to current procedures, as appropriate, to enhance accuracy and integrity of data. General Schedule routines with internal FSU team and other UD collaborators to support and manage pre and post award activities to ensure effective communication and positive working relationships. Setup and maintain project folders that will be used throughout the life of the award to maintain contract and grant documents. Manage record retention of financial data. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Accounting, Finance or a related field with two years job-related experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Knowledge of University of Delaware's PeopleSoft Grants and sponsor grant processing systems e.g., Research.Gov, CAYUSE (grants.gov), etc. preferred. Experience operating in a research intensive, higher education environment required. Ability to work independently. A high level of critical thinking, problem-solving, decision-making and attention to detail is required. Ability to interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds. Ability to communicate effectively with cross-functional, agile, collaborative teams on a variety of research management topics. Ability to handle multiple tasks simultaneously. Ability to adjust to a changing environment. Ability to develop, plan, and implement short and long-range goals. Effective analytical and technical skills are required. Experience with financial and budget planning. Skill in budget preparation and management of contracts and grants. Ability to use advanced cost analysis techniques (Excel). Ability to analyze budgetary expenditures for compliance with approved budget, and policies and procedures. Ability to gather and analyze statistical data and generate ad-hoc reports that are meaningful and understandable to those not familiar with the data being reported. Knowledge of 2 CFR 200 Uniform Guidance and other pertinent external regulations preferred. Ability to interpret, adapt and apply guidelines and procedures. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504041/research-associate-ii-biological-sciences": {
      "title": "Research Associate II, Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Research Associate II, Biological Sciences Apply now Job no: 504041 College / VP Area: College of Arts & Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Research & Laboratory Pay Grade: 29E Department: Biological Sciences College/Admin Office: Arts & Sciences Location: Newark, DE Context of Job: Under the general direction and supervision of Dr. Tanis, the Research Associate II will participate in research to identify mechanisms that regulate the shedding and uptake of extracellular vesicles from neurons utilizing the model organism C. elegans . The Research Associate will use a wide range of imaging, genetic, cell biological and biochemical approaches. The researcher will conceptualize, design and carry out experiments, maintain a notebook describing experimental procedures and results, analyze resulting data, and create figures. Major Responsibilities: Plan and conduct experiments using C. elegans that require an understanding of genetics; analyze data and interpret results Operate basic laboratory equipment such as light microscopes, fluorescent microscopes, centrifuges, pH meters, analytical balances, spectrophotometers and gel documentation systems Perform live animal confocal imaging and analysis of acquired images with Imaris and ImageJ software. Design and create new C. elegans strains using CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing. Perform molecular biology and biochemical techniques including PCR, qRT-PCR, Western blotting, and co-immunoprecipitation. Utilize information taken from primary journal articles to carry out relevant experiments and interpret results. Present experimental results to the lab group for discussion and future planning. Communicate and work with graduate and undergraduate students in the research lab. Ensure proper use and maintenance of laboratory equipment, making minor adjustments to equipment when necessary; develop protocols to assist lab members in proper use of equipment. Comply with Department of Environmental Health and Safety, OSHA, and biohazard safety standards through proper handling of potentially hazardous chemical and biological agents; promote workplace safety. Maintain laboratory strain and primer databases, monitor inventory levels, and order supplies in accordance with established policies. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Biological Sciences or a related field and two years of related experience, or equivalent combination of education. Experience working in a laboratory on an independent research project with cellular and molecular biology techniques. Knowledge and skill working with Microsoft suite (Excel, Word, PowerPoint), plasmid design, DNA sequencing and image analysis software. Experience with confocal microscopy preferred. Knowledge of the use and maintenance of laboratory facilities and/or equipment. Must have a background in genetics. Effective organizational skills and oral communication skills. Ability to understand, follow, and enforce safety procedures; ability to design, organize, and coordinate scientific and/or engineering research projects; as well as ability to develop and follow research methodology and protocol. Ability to interact well with people of all ages and diverse backgrounds. Environmental/Occupational Exposures: Work environment may include working in a cold room for short periods of time. Frequently required to use personal protective equipment to prevent exposure to hazardous materials. Special Requirements: Without assistance, must be able to lift up to 20 lbs. Requires dexterity in handling delicate and hazardous materials and the ability to accurately discern colors and measure laboratory materials. Must be able to work various and sometimes long hours due to the timing on experiments, including possibly weekends and evenings. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504043/physical-therapist-pelvic-health": {
      "title": "Physical Therapist, Pelvic Health",
      "description": "Physical Therapist, Pelvic Health Apply now Job no: 504043 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Health Services PAY GRADE: 31E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: Under general direction of the Director, the Staff Physical Therapist II is involved in direct patient care of patients in the University of Delaware Physical Therapy Clinic. This position primarily treats pelvic health patients, supports the clinical, academic, and service mission of the physical therapy clinic and physical therapy department. The Staff Physical Therapist II evaluates and treats patients with musculoskeletal and neuromuscular conditions and will supervise Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) students during patient care activities. The Physical Therapist II also has a role in mentoring of junior staff therapists, therapists in Residency and/or Fellowship training. This involves guidance and consultation with direct patient care, guidance on evaluation, treatment planning, clinical reasoning, and documentation standards. The incumbent practices with appropriate clinical independence while consulting with senior clinicians and leadership as needed. This role includes responsibility for timely and accurate clinical documentation, coordination of care, and communication with patients, students, and interdisciplinary partners. Additionally, this role includes participation in clinic initiatives, residency and fellowship initiatives, scholarly engagement, and departmental service. The position includes administrative responsibilities related to documentation oversight, student integration within the clinic environment, and participation in departmental committees and team initiatives. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: Provide direct patient care in the University of Delaware Physical Therapy Clinic, with a primary focus on treating pelvic health patients. Evaluate and plan physical therapy program for patients requiring physical therapy. This includes evaluation of musculoskeletal and neuromuscular deficits and the administration of appropriate physical agents, exercise, and manual therapy techniques. Clinical mentoring of Doctor of Physical Therapy Students. Provide support to physical therapy students in the evaluation and treatment of patients. Provides supervised learning and clinical application of physical therapy principles and procedures. Model professional standards and evidence-based practice in clinical settings. Actively communicate and collaborate with professionals in clinical practice, education, and professional mentoring to support their development. Complete timely and accurate documentation in the electronic medical record (EMR) system in accordance with clinic, University, and regulatory standards. Contribute to the academic, research, and scholarly mission of the department through community education, service, and dissemination of knowledge at the local or national level. Mentor junior physical therapy staff members, Residents, and/or Fellows, providing clinical consultation and guidance on diagnosis, intervention strategies, and documentation. Support development and implementation of Residency and Fellowship program initiatives. Actively contribute to the development and implementation of clinic initiatives. Perform other job-related duties as assigned, including serving as TA as needed. QUALIFICATIONS: Entry level or transitional Doctoral Degree in Physical Therapy and eligibility for licensure in Delaware. Documented pelvic health training—including hands-on training for any internal examinations and relevant clinical experience. Minimum of five (5) years of PT clinical experience required; Pelvic Health residency training, CAPP-Pelvic or board certification preferred. Experience mentoring junior-level PT clinicians and/or clinical teaching experience preferred. Board certification in area of specialty practice; preferred. Eligible for Board certification by the American Board of Physical Therapy Specialties in area of expertise; required Sitting for Specialist Certification required within two years of hire or promotion. Effective communication skills. Familiarity with electronic medical record (EMR) systems preferred. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504047/administrative-specialist-college-of-health-sciences": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist, College of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist, College of Health Sciences Apply now Job no: 504047 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support PAY GRADE: 29E CONTEXT OF THE JOB: The University of Delaware College of Health Sciences is a complex ecosystem of health-related academic departments, research centers, health programs, and clinics, serving as a hub of multidisciplinary research, education, healthcare, and public outreach. This position supports a variety of complex administrative support activities to ensure successful execution of College operations, programs, and initiatives. Reporting directly to the Chief of Staff, the Administrative Specialist, serves as a critical part of the Dean’s Office administrative team to execute daily operations and ensure seamless coordination of key functions and priorities. Supports designated members of the Dean’s leadership team including calendar management, meeting organization and preparation and serves as a key administrative HR resource for Dean’s Office units. Provides the administrative support structure for the Deputy Dean and Senior Business Officer to manage faculty lifecycle operations from recruitment forward ensuring the timely and accurate execution and compliance to designated University academic affairs processes. The Administrative Specialist regularly accesses highly sensitive, confidential information and requires the highest degree of discretion, professionalism and strict adherence to institutional, legal, and ethical confidentiality standards. MAJOR RESPONSIBILTIES: Deputy Dean/Faculty Life Cycle Support Support the Deputy Dean to manage faculty lifecycle from recruitment, through appointments, sabbatical leaves, promotion and tenure (P&T) and periodic reviews and with the direction of the Deputy Dean, ensure compliance with University policies, the Faculty Handbook, and the AAUP CBA, Create and maintain college processes that ensure these policies and procedures are accurately applied as faculty move through their careers with consistency and timeliness. Updates the College’s faculty database relative to faculty milestones including but not limited to promotion & tenure, peer/periodic review (contracts), sabbatical eligibility, retirement eligibility, appointment renewals, stop the clock, etc. Using approved HR templates, ensure all faculty life-cycle changes have required documentation and signatures and maintain online official HR files including faculty offer letters, renewal letters, retention letters, secondary, joint, administrative appointment letters. Provide required copies of all final documentation to departments and other designees as well as manage the record retention process in compliance with university policy. Process HR record updates for faculty to ensure the UD system of record is accurate including updates to associated pay. Support the annual faculty merit process through clear records and manage contract updates via the UD merit system. Administrative Support Provide administrative, calendar and meeting support to designated members of the Dean’s leadership team (e.g. the Deputy Dean, and Chief Clinical Officer). Manage procurement and purchasing of necessary supplies to support the Dean’s Office business activities within designated budget allocation. Support the Dean, designated team members and administrative team with expense reimbursements, honoraria, and purchasing card reconciliations, in collaboration with the Chief of Staff. Coordinate with the Communications and Marketing team to maintain and update the College of Health Sciences website, ensuring timely, accurate, and consistent presentation of college information and initiatives. Develop, maintain, and regularly update organizational charts for the Dean's Office and College units, ensuring accurate reporting relationships and position information. Serve as HR Liaison for the Dean’s Office, performing activities for the units that report to the Dean (i.e., employee onboarding and offboarding, timekeeping, search committee support, and new employee setup, including workspace, computer, and key access.) Manage the Dean’s Office primary communication and service channels daily, including front desk operations, Dean’s Office email management, and telephone communications, ensuring responsive and professional customer service. Performs other administrative job-related duties as needed. QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree and three years related experience, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Ability to handle multiple tasks concurrently, with an attention to detail, and skill in organizing resources and establishing and adjusting priorities in a rapidly changing environment. Ability to plan, organize and complete tasks in an efficient and timely manner, prior to deadlines. Ability to work independently, anticipate issues and proactively seek resolutions. Ability to interact effectively with a wide range of constituencies both within and outside the university, including excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to develop, plan, and implement short and long-range goals. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Demonstrates an understanding and consideration of the differing needs and concerns of individuals with varying identities, cultures and backgrounds. Effective analytical and technical skills, including the use of spreadsheets, and word-processing software programs. Effective organizational skills. Knowledge of the University’s Collective Bargaining Agreement and Faculty Handbook preferred. Experience with university systems include PeopleSoft, Cognos, Concur, and UDSIS preferred. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504051/quality-improvement-specialist-delaware-institute-for-excellence-in-early-childhood": {
      "title": "Quality Improvement Specialist, Delaware Institute for Excellence in Early Childhood",
      "description": "Quality Improvement Specialist, Delaware Institute for Excellence in Early Childhood Apply now Job no: 504051 College / VP Area: College of Educ & Human Dev Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Administrative Support, Student Affairs & Services Pay Grade: 28E Context of Job: The College of Education and Human Development is a diversified college with undergraduate and graduate programs that require a close working relationship with the state of Delaware, federal agencies, local school systems, and numerous health and service agencies. The CEHD houses the Delaware Institute for Excellence in Early Childhood (DIEEC). The DIEEC supports early childhood workforce and programming services for children aged six weeks to 12 years of age through the translation of evidence-based practices as well as the generation of new knowledge via applied research. The DIEEC serves the State and nation through the provision of professional development for early childhood educators in home-based and center-based settings, technical assistance to early care and education programs through Delaware’s quality improvement system, direct services to children and families through the New Directions Early Head Start program, and applied research in diverse early care and education settings. Under the general direction of the Program Manager, DIEEC, the Quality Improvement Specialist works directly with early care and education and/or school-age program leaders to support quality improvement efforts in alignment with Delaware’s quality improvement system. This position is a full-time, grant-funded position. Major Responsibilities: Provides on and off-site quality improvement support aligned with Delaware’s quality improvement system, to improve early care and education programming across a variety of early childhood programs, including center-based, home-based, school-age, and/or State-funded programs. Organizes and documents information related to program engagement and quality efforts. Independently utilizes, coordinates, and adapts techniques to support the cycle of continuous quality improvement, including relationship building, motivational interviewing, consultation practices, and goal setting. Coordinates, plans, and facilitates group meetings with early childhood professionals (e.g., Communities of Practice, Office Hours) Collaborates with a team of quality improvement specialists to design innovative ways to connect and collaborate with the early childhood workforce and to improve overall service delivery to early care and education and school-age programs. Remains well-educated and up to date regarding recommended early care and education practices. Supports the overall mission of DIEEC through planning, coordinating, and attending committees/workgroups as assigned. Performs other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Early Childhood Education or related discipline and two years of job-related experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Knowledge of child development, early care and education and/or school-age environments. Understanding and experience using adult learning principles. Must be detail oriented, proficient in Microsoft Office suite of tools and Google suite of tools. Effective oral and written communication skills Experience in collaboration and building partnerships as well as the ability to interact with a diverse group of individuals. Bilingual in Spanish preferred. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/504062/compliance-coordinator-student-financial-services": {
      "title": "Compliance Coordinator, Student Financial Services",
      "description": "Compliance Coordinator, Student Financial Services Apply now Job no: 504062 College / VP Area: VP for Enroll Management Work type: Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Student Affairs & Services Pay Grade: 28N Context of Job: The Student Financial Services compliance team is responsible for monitoring federal, state, and institutional regulations pertaining to financial aid programs and recommending changes/updates to internal policies and procedures. Under the direction of the Compliance Manager, the Compliance Coordinator for Scholarship and VA Benefits has primary responsibility for managing the day-to-day activities supporting gift and endowed scholarship awarding. The Coordinator also works closely with the School Certifying Official for VA benefits to coordinate and review individual student financial aid in conjunction with the administration of certified veteran benefits. This position also collaborates with and supports the Financial Aid Compliance Coordinator, participating in Return to Title IV Funds (R2T4) processing as needed. Major Responsibilities: Executes the awarding of gift and endowed scholarships in alignment with the university financial aid strategy and individual award restrictions, including coordination with academic units for annual awarding processes. Monitors both current-use and endowed scholarship budgets to ensure optimal expenditure of available award dollars. Review and participates in audit functions to ensure that scholarship awards are compliant with any fund restrictions. Partners with the Office of Development and Alumni Relations (DAR) to steward scholarship awards in alignment with donor intent per gift documentation. Coordinates with the Assistant Dean of Students and School Certifying Official to review student accounts for students eligible and certified for veteran benefits. Serves as the point of contact for financial aid-related questions pertaining to students using veteran benefits. Ensures application of institutional, state, and federal aid, in conjunction with veteran benefits is completed in compliance with institutional aid policies and federal regulations. Monitors and communicates any challenges or opportunities to improve service, information, and communication for students who receive veteran benefit support. Makes recommendations to ensure clarity and accuracy of published information supporting students using veteran benefits. Collaborates with the Financial Aid Compliance Coordinator to support Return to Title IV processing for all officially and unofficially withdrawn students. Maintains and participates in office-wide training programs to ensure high levels of customer service that maintain federal compliance and professional judgment standards. Performs miscellaneous job-related duties assigned. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in a job-related discipline with two years of relevant experience preferred, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Ability to use data to monitor and manage daily activities. Must be detail-oriented and able to complete processing with a high degree of accuracy. Effective oral and written communication skills. Ability to learn complex financial aid regulations and increase knowledge/experience working in financial aid compliance including Title IV and other federal financial aid programs preferred. Analytical, detail oriented and quality conscious. Skill effectively managing time, organizing, and prioritizing. Analytical skills and proficiency with report writing, data analysis, PeopleSoft querying, and other reporting tools including Excel and Tableau preferred. Prefer knowledge of regulations and compliance issues which affect the overall department, including but not limited to, State and federal laws governing student financial aid, billing and collection services required. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503765/part-time-patient-service-specialist-ud-health": {
      "title": "Part Time Patient Service Specialist, UD Health",
      "description": "Part Time Patient Service Specialist, UD Health Apply now Job no: 503765 College / VP Area: College of Health Sciences Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Newark, DE Categories: Health Services Job Title: Part Time Patient Service Specialist, UD Health Estimated Pay: $17-$23/hr based on experience Location: Health Sciences Complex- Newark, DE Context of Job: The Patient Services Specialist provides high-functioning administrative and operational support for UD Health and supports front- and back-office functions. The incumbent is responsible for evaluating effectiveness, identifying gaps, and recommending or implementing process improvements to ensure efficient operations and a high-quality patient experience. The incumbent applies established policies, procedures, and guidelines to address patient service and administrative issues, escalating complex matters as appropriate, and determines appropriate responses to inquiries from patients, families, leadership, insurance providers, and community partners. The position also supports patient intake processes, including medical billing information, insurance verification, and financial coordination. Operating with minimal supervision, this role requires initiative, problem-solving, and the consistent discretion and independent judgment on clinic related matters. May require travel between UD Health clinic locations based on business needs. Major Responsibilities: Independently determine and modify intake and scheduling frameworks to ensure equitable access, operational efficiency and regulatory alignment. Review, process, and verify financial and non-financial patient documentation, including processing co-pays, daily billing, reviewing patient accounts, identifying delinquent accounts, and collecting due amounts. Communicate department practices and procedures to patients and families with clarity and empathy, using active listening and supportive communication techniques. Administer and coordinate appointment scheduling utilizing EMR systems and workflows, ensuring appropriate prioritization, compliance, and efficient utilization of resources. Communicate with staff and follow special instructions related to patient appointments. Input patient documentation and add correspondence into the electronic medical record. Analyze waitlist data, access trends, and scheduling bottlenecks; generate regular reports; and recommend operational adjustments. Handle highly sensitive and confidential information. Follow HIPAA privacy rights, where applicable. Evaluate non-routine or complex service access issues and determine appropriate administrative escalation or resolution pathways. Optimize clinical workflow by managing provider schedules, analyze impact of scheduling trends on clinic productivity, implementing solutions to improve patient throughput. Assess operational gaps related to front-of-office coverage and service access and recommend adjustments to administrative workflows or coverage models as needed. Resolve administrative and service-access issues, determine appropriate responses, and independently implement solutions; escalating only issues involving policy deviation or significant institutional risk. Coordinate administrative components of projects and initiatives, including scheduling, sequencing, and resource alignment, with discretion to determine appropriate administrative approaches. Perform other job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications : High school diploma or GED and four years of administrative, healthcare support, patient services, customer service, or related experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Proficiency in Microsoft Windows, Microsoft Office (Access, Excel, PowerPoint, Word, Outlook) and Google Drive apps, e.g., docs, sheets, slides and forms. Experience with electronic medical records and scheduling. Effective interpersonal, oral and written communication skills. Effective organizational skills, attention to detail; ability to manage high-volume workflows. Ability to collaborate and work successfully in a team environment. Ability to ensure compliance with established policies, procedures, and regulatory requirements. Knowledge of federally mandated policies. Ability to analyze administrative operations, identify risks or inefficiencies, and recommend or implement process improvements. Experience in data collection, including collecting, compiling and maintaining data from multiple sources such as files, records, databases, staff or others. Ability to handle confidential information. Special Requirements: May require schedule flexibility to support operational needs. Ability to travel to various clinic locations to support patient volume and coverage fluctuations as needed. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.udel.edu/cw/en-us/job/503980/parttime-exam-proctor-ud-online": {
      "title": "Part-Time Exam Proctor, UD Online",
      "description": "Part-Time Exam Proctor, UD Online Apply now Job no: 503980 College / VP Area: Office of the Provost Work type: Part-Time Staff Location: Georgetown, DE Categories: Administrative Support Job Title: Part Time Exam Proctor Anticipated Start Date: October 22, 2026 Estimated Pay: $15.30 per hour Location: Delaware Tech Jack F. Owens Campus, Jason Building, Room 182; 18800 Seashore Highway, Georgetown, DE. Position Description: Supervise student examinations; communicate and ensure the students testing are following UD Online Resource Center (Newark, Delaware) procedures and the exam guidelines of the instructor. Requirements: Strong organizational and communication skills, attention to detail, proficiency with computers. Must be able to interact effectively with students from a variety of diverse backgrounds. Strong interpersonal skills are required as the successful candidate will need to work effectively with all members of the University community including colleagues, staff, administrators and students served by PCS. Must be able to work in a fast-paced environment and to keep/maintain confidential materials, files and information securely. Must be reliable and dependable with working assigned hours. Flexibility, experience in a higher education setting and/or with students are considered pluses. Cannot be a student. Duties: Maintain flexibility with scheduling, available to work afternoon and evening shifts. Assertive yet professional behavior to handle academic dishonesty should it arise. Administer exams using computer software and various technology. Calm demeanor during computer glitches and/or stressful situations. Ability to work well independently and/or as a team. Interact effectively with all students regarding test administration and behaviors. Communicate with administrator regularly and always be reliable and honest. Position Details: This is a 12-month, part-­‐time, no more than 10 hour per week, miscellaneous wage non-benefitted position. Candidate must be available on Thursday afternoons and some Wednesdays in late Spring, Summer. We are located at the Delaware Tech Jack F. Owens Campus, Jason Building, Room 182; 18800 Seashore Highway, Georgetown, DE. Notice of Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity The University of Delaware does not discriminate against any person on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, genetic information, marital status, disability, religion, age, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by applicable law in its employment, educational programs and activities, admissions policies, and scholarship and loan programs as required by Title IX of the Educational Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, and other applicable statutes and University policies. The University of Delaware also prohibits unlawful harassment including sexual harassment and sexual violence. Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
